\par\par \b \uc2 IMF\uc2  Expands Aid Package for Argentina -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 937 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2018\par 01:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Jeffrey T. Lewis and Ryan Dube \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  agreed to boost its bailout package signed with Argentina in June, providing additional financial support and accelerating the disbursement of funds as the administration of embattled President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mauricio Macri\f28 \uc2 \f2  confronts market turbulence and social unrest.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Argentina Finance Minister \uc2 Nicolas Dujovne\uc2  and IMF Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  said Wednesday that the new agreement will increase the bailout size by almost 15% to $57.1 billion, providing an extra $19 billion in financing through the end of 2019, when Mr. Macri's term ends. It commits \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintaining a floating exchange-rate regime without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The Fund remains fully committed to helping \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  tackle the problems that it faces," Ms. Lagarde said during a joint news conference with Mr. Dujovne in New York.\par \par \uc2 The deal seeks to address concerns about the country's ability to service its rising debt load and secure funds to cover government spending.\par \par \uc2 "This will permit our country to leave behind the path of turbulence we've been exposed to recently and return the Argentine economy to the structural conditions we need for stable growth," Mr. Dujovne said.\par \par \uc2 The deal still needs to be approved by the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's board to take effect.\par \par \uc2 The expanded program will aim to reduce the country's double-digit inflation by replacing inflation targets with monetary base goals to contain the money supply, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  said. It also will require the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain its high interest rate, which was increased to 60% in August.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be able to conduct limited \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market only if there is "extreme overshooting of the exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market when the exchange rate is between 34 pesos and 44 pesos to the U.S. dollar, Argentina's new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Guido Sandleris said later on Wednesday. The peso closed weaker Wednesday at 38.52 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Macri was forced to ask the \uc2 IMF\uc2  for help earlier this year, after rising interest rates in the U.S. pummeled the peso and investors sold assets in emerging markets. But the aid package failed to contain the peso's steady weakening, forcing the government in August to ask for more \uc2 IMF\uc2  funds and faster disbursements. The Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost half of its value against the dollar so far this year.\par \par \uc2 "I don't imagine Argentina will be able to tap external markets again very soon, after all they're going to have a very deep recession, high inflation, uncertainty about the election next year, so there are still a lot of fundamental questions about how stable the macro situation is going to be," said Gustavo Rangel, Latin America's chief economist for ING. "It will take some time for them to really be able to access the market again."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Dujovne then announced austerity measures in early September, including deep cuts to subsidies and public works as part of an effort to balance a wide budget shortfall and meet \uc2 IMF\uc2  conditions.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  deal comes a day after Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, \uc2 Luis Caputo\uc2 , unexpectedly resigned less than four months into the job amid reports of disputes with the fund over the bank's exchange rate policy. Under Mr. Caputo, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, burning billions of dollars in reserves to contain the peso selloff. The \uc2 IMF\uc2  objected to that, backing a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He was replaced by Mr. Sandleris, a top Finance Ministry official who was educated at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 . On Tuesday, Mr. Sandleris said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main objective is to reduce inflation, currently at 34%.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sandleris said at a press conference that the central institution will ensure zero nominal growth in the monetary base -- bills and coins in circulation and banks' deposits at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- between now and next June in its effort to bring inflation down, which he added implies a significant monetary contraction.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's economy contracted 2.7% in July from the year-earlier month as the country's financial crisis hit consumer demand. In the second quarter, the economy fell 4.2% from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 The economic crisis has rattled Mr. Macri's administration as it faces growing anger among Argentines upset with the sharp contraction and high inflation. On Tuesday, Argentina's powerful unions held a national strike, warning that it would increase protests if the government doesn't reverse austerity measures.\par \par \uc2 "If there isn't a plan B, there won't be a truce with Argentina's union movement," said union leader Juan Carlos Schmid.\par \par \uc2 After taking office in late 2015, Mr. Macri had hoped to avoid social upheaval by gradually unraveling his predecessor's free-spending policies rather than implementing the kind of economic shock therapies that sparked unrest and led to the downfall of previous presidents. His government relied heavily on foreign credit to plug the deficit, raising concerns among economists as the cost of servicing the debt now increases due to the weaker peso.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's debt ratio will reach 84% of gross domestic product by the end of the year, compared with 57% a year earlier, consultancy Oxford Economics said this week. "Argentina's debt-to-GDP ratio is rising at an unsustainable pace," the firm said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jeffrey T. Lewis at jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com and Ryan Dube at ryan.dube@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 26, 2018 19:12 ET (23:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180926ee9q003gh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
