\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Sees Inflation Declining on New Policies -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 158 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2018\par 00:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 2230 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] -- Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, Guido Sandleris, says it could take a few months for inflation to start declining after the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  scrapped inflation targets in favor of monetary base goals to contain the supply of money. "We are attacking the root cause of inflation," Sandleris says. "The objective is that it will decline as fast as possible." The change in policy comes as the \uc2 IMF\uc2  boosted its bailout package for Argentina to $57.1B from $50B. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also committed to not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market when the exchange rate is between 34 pesos to 44 pesos to the USD, Sandleris says. \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were a cause of conflict with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , which supports a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 26, 2018 18:36 ET (22:36 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180926ee9q003ig\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
