\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 New president of Central Argentina is aligned with \uc2 IMF\uc2  policy\b0\par\par\uc2 453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2018\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Advocates of a new agreement, Argentine President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  and IMF Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  dined in New York on Monday night. Only it was enough that twelve hours of the meeting happened so that the resignation was made public \uc2 Luis Caputo\uc2  to the headquarters of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BCRA), that during its short management incessantly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  the market to avoid that the dollar rose, without success nor endorsement of the Background. In his place comes Guido Sandleris, closer to the vision of the multilateral organization and defender of the exchange rate float.\par \par \uc2 Although it is a country accustomed to changes in the head of the Central - only one president has finished his term in 83 years of history and the previous one, \uc2 Federico Sturzenegger\uc2 , resigned just three months ago - the news did not fall well in the market. Today, the dollar reached around $ 40 Argentine pesos and although it moderated its fall during the day, closing at $ 38.2 was the most depreciated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world.\par \par \uc2 The "personal reasons" with which BCRA justified the departure of Caputo did not convince. According to Marcos Buscaglia, founding partner of Alberdi Partners and former chief economist of Bank of America, "Caputo wanted more firepower to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market, there was even talk of implementing a system of exchange bands, as Chile had in the nineties, But the \uc2 IMF\uc2  is looking for something else, a floating exchange rate. "\par \par \uc2 Also in conversation with La Tercera , Martin Alfie, chief economist of Radar Consultora, argues that "the \uc2 IMF\uc2  with its conditions already took two presidents of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (...) It is a total loss of sovereignty of the management of exchange policy."\par \par \uc2 Indeed, until today the main advisor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Guido Sandleris is a declared defender of the model that currently operates in our country. "Having a floating exchange rate has its cost, which is to lower inflation slower, but adjust to external shocks and cushion the impacts on the economy," he told Infobae in May.\par \par \uc2 Today, the new \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said in a brief statement that "the main objective of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to reduce inflation." But although he wants to turn the page, without a new agreement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  on the table, the debate continues. "A floating exchange rate can work in developed economies or emerging markets with a long history of stability, but not in Argentina. I think the idea of ??having bands is better, "said Mat\u237\'00\'EDas Carugati, chief economist at Argentine consultancy Management & Fit.\par \par \uc2 The entry New president of Central Argentina is aligned with \uc2 IMF\uc2  policy was first published in La Tercera.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020180925ee9p0050s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
