\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 5- \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  peso firms as  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 IMF\uc2  voices support\b0\par\par\uc2 761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2018\par 22:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds S&P negative credit outlook, paragraphs 4, 15-16)\par \par \uc2 By Scott Squires and Jorge Otaola\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Aug 31 (Reuters) - Argentina's beleaguered peso bounced back slightly on Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctioned $250 million in dollar reserves and the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  issued a strong statement of support for President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 's government.\par \par \uc2 But with frustration rising over inflation of more than 30 percent and austerity measures required under a $50 billion \uc2 IMF\uc2  standby deal, Agriculture Ministry employees protested budget cutbacks and members of the opposition Citizens' Unity party called for countrywide marches against Macri Friday evening.\par \par \uc2 The party's leader, former President \uc2 Cristina Fernandez\uc2 , a proponent of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy, may run against Macri for president next year, raising further questions about the country's economic prospects. She has, however, been tarred by a graft scandal marked by the jailing of several former officials.\par \par \uc2 Macri is caught between voters tired of fiscal belt-tightening measures already implemented and investor pressure to unveil further spending cuts. Ratings agency S&P said it revised its outlook on Argentina to negative from stable due to risks that the government may not be able to implement its economic plan.\par \par \uc2 "We lost our jobs because of the government's orthodox, fundamentalist policies," said Jorge Harvez, 63, an office worker with the agriculture ministry who was fired on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 One of about 250 protesters blocking traffic in front of the ministry's main office in Buenos Aires, Harvez blamed layoffs on the austerity push.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue blocking the streets until we force the ministers to negotiate with us," he said.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman said the Agriculture Ministry had laid off 548 people, most in a division that assists small-scale farmers, "to make more efficient use of state resources."\par \par \uc2 UNPOPULAR IMF\par \par \uc2 Many Argentines blame the \uc2 IMF\uc2  for budget cuts that plunged millions into poverty during the 2001-02 economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 The peso strengthened 6.08 percent on Friday to close at 37 to the dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would auction $675 million in foreign reserves. On balance the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost 25.89 percent against the greenback in August and 49.59 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 It lost 20 percent on Wednesday and Thursday alone after Macri said he would ask the \uc2 IMF\uc2  to advance disbursements under a $50 billion financing deal. The announcement raised alarms among investors that Argentina might be hard pressed to fund its 2019 deficit.\par \par \uc2 "Argentina has the full support of the Fund and we are confident that the strong commitment and determination of the Argentine authorities will help the country overcome the current difficulties," IMF spokesman \uc2 Gerry Rice\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The statement helped the peso stabilize, at least temporarily, said Ignacio Labaqui, an analyst for New York consultancy Medley Global Advisors.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 IMF\uc2  support is definitely helpful at this stage," he said.\par \par \uc2 In its statement, S&P said it maintained its credit rating for Argentina at B+ - several notches below investment grade - because Macri's commitment to austerity measures should help sustain investor confidence. But it said the recent depreciation in the peso threatened to keep inflation high.\par \par \uc2 SPENDING CUTS\par \par \uc2 The government is expected to announce a policy package containing further spending cuts. But more belt tightening will meet strong opposition, as discontent with Macri grows over high inflation, a weak economy and subsidy cuts that have jacked up household heating and water bills.\par \par \uc2 With more austerity on the way, and utility prices set to rise again in the fourth quarter, low and middle income Argentines are hard-pressed to pay household costs. Yet the \uc2 IMF\uc2  and investors want more fiscal tightening.\par \par \uc2 "If the government fails to deliver a convincing austerity package, the peso will probably slide further and another large interest rate hike would be likely," consultancy Capital Economics said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already hiked its monetary policy rate to 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 IMF Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  and Finance Minister \uc2 Nicolas Dujovne\uc2  were scheduled to meet on Tuesday "to advance the dialogue," Rice said.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to reporters on Thursday night, Dujovne said the government would announce new economic measures on Monday, and would target a 2019 primary fiscal deficit below the 1.3 percent of gross domestic product agreed with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 . (Additional reporting by Hugh Bronstein, Luc Cohen, Gabriel Burin and Rodrigo Campos in New York Editing by Leslie Adler, Steve Orlofsky and David Gregorio) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-8-31T21:18:06.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180831ee8v028mu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
