\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil stocks slide, Mexico stocks and peso rise\b0\par\par\uc2 486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2018\par 02:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Updates prices)\par     SAO PAULO, June 8 (Reuters) - Brazilian equities extended\par their recent slide amid political and fiscal worries on Friday,\par while Mexican stocks and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  advanced.\par     Stocks on the benchmark Bovespa stock index tumbled\par across the board for a fourth straight session, as traders\par continued to fret ahead of an unpredictable October election in\par which market-friendly candidates have failed to gain traction,\par according to recent polls.\par     Recent government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in oil company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petroleo\par Brasileiro SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  has also caused traders to question the\par resolve of the current government - and the ability of the next\par one - to stay on a path of pro-market policies and relative\par fiscal austerity.\par     "It's still an uncertain scenario, without a positive\par conclusion coming to light for local financial markets," said a\par Rio de Janeiro-based trader.\par     The Bovespa index was down 1.23 percent in late morning\par trade, after falling almost 3 percent earlier in the session.\par     After a punishing week for the Mexican peso, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par recovered on Friday, up 1.06 percent in afternoon trading.\par Investors are closely watching the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Group of Seven\f28 \uc2 \f2  summit in\par Canada and were encouraged by the fact that U.S. President\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  did not intensify his rhetoric on trade, analysts\par said. \par     Mexico's benchmark index IPC was up 1.02 percent in\par afternoon trading.\par     In neighboring Argentina, the peso fell sharply\par against the dollar after the country signed a $50 billion\par standby financing arrangement with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary\par Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 , causing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to abandon its spirited defense\par of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "I think (the) USD/ARS upward trend should remain intact,"\par said Win Thin, Global Head of Emerging Market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategy\par at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\par     "Once the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  deal was reached in principle, I'm sure that\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Argentina\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was asked to take \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  off the table. Last\par thing the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  wants to see is its program money burned away by\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par     The Argentine peso was down 1.25 percent at 25.3 per dollar\par in late afternoon trading.\par     \par Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 2015 GMT:  \par     \par  Stock indexes             Latest          Daily   YTD pct\par                                              pct    change\par                                           change  \par  MSCI Emerging Markets        1,137.69     -1.04     -0.76\par                                                   \par  MSCI LatAm                   2,510.55       2.5    -13.39\par                                                   \par  Brazil Bovespa              72,942.07     -1.23     -4.53\par  Mexico IPC                  45,939.54      1.02     -6.92\par  Chile IPSA                   5,510.67      0.78     -0.97\par  Chile IGPA                  27,885.75      0.79     -0.34\par  Argentina MerVal            31,454.66      4.23      4.62\par  Colombia IGBC               12,251.57     -0.09      7.75\par  Venezuela IBC               40,543.53      1.71  3,109.74\par  \par  (Reporting by Gram Slattery; additional reporting by Paula\par Arend Laier in Sao Paulo, Rodrigo Campos in New York, and Sheky\par Espejo; editing by G Crosse and Diane Craft)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-6-9T01:08:41.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180609ee69000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
