\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The keys to understanding the financial crisis in Argentina\b0\par\par\uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2018\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 For weeks, Argentines have watched anxiously how the peso falls against the dollar. The news that the liberal president \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  seeks the help of the \uc2 IMF\uc2  revived old ghosts of crisis and painful adjustments. However, the government is committed to sending a reassuring message and ensures that Argentina is a solvent country. What led Argentina to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and financial crisis, and what does it need to conjure it? Lee: \uc2 Moody's\uc2  doubt Argentina crisis contagion to Latin America Chronic deficit Analysts agree that the main problem of the Argentine economy is its fiscal deficit (3.9% of GDP in 2017) and its trade balance (8,470 million dollars in 2017). For the past two and a half years Macri has been developing a program of adjustments considered "gradualist" that tries to bring the country from the protectionist model of the governments of N\u233\'00\'E9stor and \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  (2003-2015) to one of openness.\par \par \uc2  Among its first measures, it eliminated exchange control and negotiated with speculative funds to get the country out of default. This allowed him to finance the deficit with credits from abroad for amounts of about 30,000 million dollars per year. Lee: \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  performs strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Ramiro Casti\u241\'00\'F1eira, an analyst at the Econometric firm, argues that Macri "assumed a broken country, with great effort, he made progress, such as getting out of default, getting out of restrictions and recovering economic growth, but he could not resolve the structural: the deficit." Room for maneuver Many Argentines remember these days the economic and social crisis of 2001, which caused the fall of four presidents in a week and led the country to declare the largest default in history. And although there are similarities between the two moments, such as the accelerated depreciation of the peso and the request for help from the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , the experts stress that there are differences that grant a margin of maneuver to the Macri government. In 2001, the "1 to 1" rule was in effect, as was the case of the fixed-rate exchange rate policy of a peso equal to one dollar. Instead, at this time the floating exchange rate and the autonomous monetary policy "should facilitate an adjustment that prevents the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis from escalating to a banking crisis and ending in an economic depression," according to Edward Glossop of \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "An autonomous monetary policy allows the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to increase interest rates to prop up capital flows, while at the same time weakening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would help stabilize, restore competitiveness and make local assets more attractive," he said. Glossop. Gradualism The motto of the Macri government has been "gradualism". That is, make adjustments little by little so as not to cause a social shock or a level of conflict that affects governance. Thus, it gradually eliminated subsidies to public services tariffs, opened the country to imports and reduced spending, but without achieving balance yet. The withdrawal of subsidies for services such as gas, electricity or water made, in some cases astronomically, the cost of households whose purchasing power is eaten by an inflation of more than 20% per year. We recommend: \uc2 IMF\uc2  support for Argentina is bad news, says former finance minister Marcos Pe\u241\'00\'F1a, chief of the Cabinet, said: "We can not continue thinking that the fault lies with another, the fault of which we spend more than what we have is of the Argentines". Can you get out of the crisis? The government stresses that it went to the \uc2 IMF\uc2  toprevent a major crisis. Although the amount of aid that you will receive has not yet been defined, it is taken for granted that the multilateral entity will put some conditions. "The \uc2 IMF\uc2  is going to rethink gradualism," predicts Casti\u241\'00\'F1eira. "Argentina must be aware of the situation in which it finds itself in order to make the necessary adjustment and not have to borrow abroad," he said. For Marina dal Poggetto, of EcoGo, "not depend on external credit requires a very violent adjustment, which is politically complicated." We recommend: How does the stand-by agreement that Argentina asks the \uc2 IMF\uc2 ? Looking ahead to a 2019 marked by presidential elections and without a parliamentary majority, the task looks uphill.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020180516ee5g003n4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
