\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS- \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2 's peso slides again;  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2018\par 18:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     SAO PAULO/BUENOS AIRES, May 11 (Reuters) - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Argentina\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's peso\par tumbled to a record low against the dollar on Friday, causing\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot market for a second\par straight day, as talks for an \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par financing line failed to calm traders.\par     On Thursday, IMF Director \f28 \uc2 \f2 Christine Lagarde\f28 \uc2 \f2  said she\par instructed her team to continue discussions regarding a\par fund-supported program for Argentina following an extremely\par rocky few weeks for the peso. Since March, the peso has slid\par over 10 percent. In May alone, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par registered intraday slides of over 5 percent and gains of over 2\par percent.\par     The Argentine government has hiked the benchmark interest\par rate some 1,275 basis points in recent weeks and announced it\par would seek a so-called "high access stand-by" agreement from the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  to stabilize the country's economy.\par     While rhetoric has been sanguine, there are no concrete\par commitments as of yet.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Argentine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot market for\par the second consecutive day on Friday, but that did not stop the\par peso's slide. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was trading down 4.18 percent in\par afternoon trade.\par     Investors "are continuing to unwind their positions (in\par peso-denominated assets) to dollarize (their portfolios)," said\par a Buenos Aires-based trader. "The central back had to sell\par dollars once again, but they were absorbed by the market\par immediately and the exchange rate didn't fall."\par     Across Latin America, some other major markets saw their\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weaken over 1 percent as well.\par     In Brazil, the real \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was trading down 1.31\par percent against the dollar. Traders said they were adopting\par defensive positions ahead of a new presidential election poll.\par     "Markets are hoping for a candidate with a reformist vision\par rising in the polls, which hasn't happened yet," wrote brokerage\par Advanced Corretora in a report.\par     In Mexico, the peso fell 1.02 percent after Mexican\par Economy Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ildefonso Guajardo\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the nation would not be\par rushed into a poor \f28 \uc2 \f2 North American Free Trade Agreement\f28 \uc2 \f2  deal and\par that plenty of issues with the negotiations were outstanding.\par  \par     \par Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1537 GMT:\par \par  Stock indexes                           daily %     YTD %\par                              Latest       change    change\par  MSCI Emerging Markets         1164.07      0.65     -0.16\par                                                   \par  MSCI LatAm                    2869.76     -0.61       2.1\par                                                   \par  Brazil Bovespa               85668.03     -0.22     12.13\par                                                   \par  Mexico IPC                   46580.17      0.06     -5.62\par       