\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Argentine peso weakens to record closing low of 20.55 per U.S. dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 171 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2018\par 21:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds Thursday peso weakening and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, April 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened 1.41 percent to an all-time closing low of 20.55 per U.S. dollar on Thursday, despite massive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the trading session and on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold about $1 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday, local traders said. The day before it sold $1.472 billion on the local spot market, its largest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in at least 15 years.\par \par \uc2 The bank has frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the peso in recent months as a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could spur inflation in a country that has long lived with double-digit increases in consumer prices. It has sold more than $4 billion since March. (Reporting by Walter Bianchi, writing by Hugh Bronstein Editing by Chizu Nomiyama and Lisa Shumaker) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-4-26T20:05:59.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180426ee4q00pma\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
