\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Puzzles Foreign Airlines -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Taos Turner \par \uc2 1,023 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Dezember 2017\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A16\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (December 26, 2017).\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES -- Argentina's pro-business President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  is inviting low-cost airlines to invest in Argentina's uncompetitive air-travel market, as long as they do something they do nowhere else: keep their prices high.\par \par \uc2 That caveat is baffling not only to the companies, but also to the many Argentines, like 61-year-old Lu\u237\'00\'EDs Segato, who badly want to see more domestic flights in the world's eighth-largest nation.\par \par \uc2 "Argentina is the strangest country in the world. We are capitalist in our rhetoric but communist in our practices," said the Buenos Aires resident, who mostly uses buses to travel around the country training firefighters. "How else can you explain why a pro-market government would tell airlines how much they can charge for tickets?"\par \par \uc2 Mr. Macri has slashed taxes, subsidies and red tape in an effort to make Argentina more attractive for investors. But when it comes to air travel and many other problematic zones of the country's economy, he has run into hard political realities. The country's powerful unions, secure in a long tradition of state protection that reaches back to the 1940s era of former President Juan Domingo Per\u243\'00\'F3n, are often not amused by notions of change.\par \par \uc2 The Macri administration has given Norwegian Air Argentina and Flybondi, a local startup, approval for new flights to scores of cities at lower fares -- but not too low. The newcomers say they could offer seats like those sold in Europe for as little as $15 -- a quarter the cost of a typical 13-hour bus ride -- if Mr. Macri would only let them.\par \par \uc2 The reason he isn't, government officials and industry executives say, is that allowing low-cost carriers to compete too freely might destroy bus-driver jobs and hurt dominant carrier \uc2 Aerol\u237\'00\'EDneas Argentinas\uc2 , a state-run behemoth with about 12,000 employees.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a long history of heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its economy. Until last year, it set caps and minimum prices on airfares and made it hard for foreign carriers to operate here. In 2009, Mr. Macri's predecessor, \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2 , a firebrand nationalist, expropriated Aerol\u237\'00\'EDneas from a troubled Spanish travel company.\par \par \uc2 "It seems a bit crazy that in a country that's suffering from inflation you've got a company that wants to lower prices and isn't allowed to," said Julian Cook, chief executive of Flybondi, which aims to begin offering domestic and international flights next year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Macri has feverishly overhauled the economy in two years in office, enacting drastic measures to fight double-digit inflation, lifting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls and reaching a debt deal with foreign creditors. More recently, Mr. Macri moved to change Argentina's pension system, sparking violent street protests.\par \par \uc2 In some areas he faces steep resistance from strident union bosses, who say market-oriented policies displace workers and erode salaries.\par \par \uc2 Last year, after Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  encouraged banks to start emailing digital statements instead of sending them off in paper envelopes, a truckers' union threatened to stop hauling cash to automated teller machines, citing fears that digital statements would mean less work. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  delayed its plans.\par \par \uc2 Such resistance highlights a constraint on Mr. Macri's ability to modernize a country where many favor state protection over market competition.\par \par \uc2 "If they let these low-cost carriers in it will be raining cadavers," said Pablo Bir\u243\'00\'F3, head of the pilots union, claiming budget airlines wouldn't invest properly in maintenance.\par \par \uc2 Bus-driver unions also fear the worst. Roberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez, head of the UTA transportation workers union, says budget airlines would deprive long-distance buses of passengers, killing up to 10,000 ground-transportation jobs. "Macri needs to tell me where those people are going to find work," Mr. Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez said.\par \par \uc2 Airline unions have repeatedly shut down airports nationwide, canceling hundreds of flights and leaving thousands of passengers stranded.\par \par \uc2 But carriers like Norwegian are undeterred and see opportunity in Argentina, which has Latin America's biggest middle class but where people are only half as likely to fly as residents of countries with similar income levels, according to Aeropuertos Argentina 2000, which runs 35 airports in Argentina.\par \par \uc2 "We see the Argentine market the way other markets were 20 years ago," said Ole Cristian Melhus, the regional head of Norwegian Air Argentina, which says it wants to invest up to $4.3 billion here.\par \par \uc2 Holger Paulmann, chief executive of Sky Airline, a budget carrier based in Chile, says he would offer domestic flights here -- but only if he could set his own prices.\par \par \uc2 "The minimum fare in Argentina is probably eight to 10 times higher than the lowest fare in Chile," Mr. Paulmann said.\par \par \uc2 Norwegian could eventually offer one-way tickets from Buenos Aires to Mendoza, home of Argentina's famous Malbec vineyards, for just $15, Mr. Melhus said. The government-set minimum price is currently about $58.\par \par \uc2 "We are carrying out an airline revolution, but it's a gradual process," said Guillermo Dietrich, Argentina's transportation minister.\par \par \uc2 There are other hurdles for airlines. Pilots are required to be Argentine citizens, and there aren't enough of them. Mr. Cook estimates that up to 400 Argentine pilots work abroad, and says he is trying to entice them to work for Flybondi.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, jet fuel costs about 15% more in Argentina than in Chile or the U.S. Duties to import a new airplane are high, Mr. Paulmann says. And a single company, Intercargo, has a nationwide monopoly on airport services, raising operating costs.\par \par \uc2 Brazil and Colombia, which opened up their airline markets, attracted millions of new passengers who never flew before, said Andy Ricover, a transportation industry expert.\par \par \uc2 "Who's opposed to that? The unions are holding back Argentina's development by opposing deregulation," he said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Taos Turner at taos.turner@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 26, 2017 02:32 ET (07:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020171226edcq0008j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4500\pichgoal375
