\par\par \b \uc2 Market Talk Roundup: Latest on Trump, U.S. Politics\b0\par\par\uc2 1,522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2017\par 22:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1607 ET - The Dow rises 15 points to 21859, the S&P adds 0.1% to 2441 and the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  jumps 0.6% to 6257, but all three are down more than 1% for the week, which was marred by escalating tensions between the US and North Korea. Tech stocks are the best performing S&P sector, but \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  is the index's worst performing stock, down 5.3%, after posting earnings Thursday afternoon. \uc2 TripAdvisor\uc2  leads gainers, up 6.2%. Crude oil gains 0.4% to $48.78. Gold rises 0.5% to $1297. Treasury yields fall to 2.190% from 2.211% Thursday. The US dollar hit its lowest level against the Japanese yen in months at Y109.09, and the WSJ Dollar Index fell 0.3%. (jonathan.vuocolo@wsj.com; @jonvuocolo)\par \par \uc2 1501 ET - Republicans' failure to overhaul the Affordable Care Act has made it increasingly unlikely the administration will be able to push through pro-growth policies that will supercharge US economic growth, an S&P Global Ratings economist says. "We no longer believe the federal government will be able to push through even a small infrastructure-spending package--much less the $1 trillion the White House has suggested," says Beth Ann Bovino, US chief economist. As for tax cuts, Bovino expects the GOP will be able to push through a "small tax cut of $500 billion, rather than true tax reform." Taken together, the dimming outlook for policy changes suggests economic growth will stay stuck around 2%, below the 3% target Trump has highlighted, she adds. (akane.otani@wsj.com; @akaneotani)\par \par \uc2 1158 ET - London's top flight looks set to end the session firmly in the red as Trump fired his latest salvo across the bows of North Korea. The FTSE 100 Index falls 1%, or 73.89 to 7316 as Trump declared that the US is "locked and loaded should North Korea act unwisely." The high drama is prompting traders to pull cash from stocks and invest it in safer assets like gold, says David Madden at \uc2 CMC Markets\uc2 . "For the time being, tensions seem to be going one way and equity markets are losing ground because of it," he says. The gold price is up 0.2% at $1,292.60. Gold miner Randgold cashes in, up 0.1%, though other miners fall on general economic uncertainty caused by the stand-off. Housebuilders rise after Ibstock reported better-than-expected first-half earnings, with Persimmon up 1.5%. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1147 ET - Germany's DAX ends slightly down at 12014, with traders cautious due to the stand-off between U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and North Korea. German Chancellor \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  Friday says Germany supports non-military solutions to the crisis and that rhetorical escalation is the 'wrong answer'. German stocks broadly hold ground despite the caution, with Frankfurt traders noting firm buying support around that level. Health-care group \uc2 Fresenius SE & Co\uc2  closes as the biggest winner, up 1.75% as it recovers ground lost following earnings data Thursday. (william.wilkes@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1116 ET - Argentina's construction industry will expand at a faster-than-expected pace this year and next, BMI Research says in a report. "We have upgraded our expectations for construction industry real growth in 2017 and 2018 to 6.1% and 4.6%, up from 2.3% and 4.4% respectively," BMI says, citing increased government investment in public works. BMI says private sector investment will also be an important driver in 2018 as the government pushes new public-private partnerships. China will also play a key role in infrastructure development, BMI says, referring to agreements signed between the countries earlier this year. China has shown keen interest in Argentina's nuclear and hydropower generation sectors. There is a risk to growth, though, and her name is \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2 , the former president who is running for a Senate seat. A strong Kirchner victory in October's midterm election "could weaken investor sentiment," BMI says. (taos.turner@wsj.com; @taos)\par \par \uc2 1051 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening past 18 pesos to the US dollar. But early Friday, in the last day of trading before a primary election this Sunday, the market was calm. "Remarkable tranquility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, with the wholesale dollar trading smoothly at 17.68 to 17.70," Gustavo Quintana, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader, says in a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  post. In June, investors moved to buy more dollars after former president \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  confirmed plans to run for Argentina's Senate. Her candidacy sparked concern she could regain political clout and hurt President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 's chances of continuing to overhaul Argentina's economy. Some analysts say the peso could slide past 19 if she decisively wins a senate seat in a midterm election this October. In that event, investors would seek financial security by buying dollars, analysts say. (taos.turner@wsj.com; @taos)\par \par \uc2 0943 ET - The \uc2 CBOE\uc2  Volatility Index shed 6.4% shortly after the opening bell, taking an about-turn after shooting up to its highest level since Election Day on Thursday. The VIX, which measures investors' expectations for swings in the S&P 500, has rocketed higher this week as investors have weighed escalating tensions between North Korea and the US. The index is still up 50% for the week--on course for its biggest one-week gain since December 2015. (akane.otani@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0854 ET - Treasurys strengthen after CPI numbers miss expectations. Both headline and core CPI in July climbed 0.1% vs. 0.2% anticipated by economists polled by WSJ. Continued soft inflation should make it harder for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates and diminishes the threat of a big selloff in Treasurys anytime soon. Treasurys had already rallied overnight on concerns over North Korea, but had pared gains just before the CPI release. The 10-year yield was recently 2.191% vs. 2.211% Thursday. (sam.goldfarb@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0849 ET - US stock futures climb into positive territory after data shows inflation in the US remained subdued in July, extending a weak streak that's persisted throughout the year. Futures for the S&P 500 point to a 0.1% opening gain while Dow Jones Industrial Average futures tick up less than 0.1%. The moves come even after President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  issues his starkest threat yet to North Korea, warning the country that "military solutions" are "in place, locked and loaded." One possible reason why: soft inflation has been a big question mark for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  this year, and investors have bet that continued weakness will keep the Fed from aggressively dialing back easy-money policies that have helped stocks keep climbing. (akane.otani@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0728 ET - S&P futures are off 3% and Dow futures are also lower as investors pare back risk amid escalating tensions between the US and North Korea. The Dow closed down 205 points Thursday, in its biggest decline since May 17, after President Trump said his recent warning to North Korea about threatening the US "maybe wasn't tough enough." In corporate news, SNAP is down 15% premarket after late yesterday reporting that its quarterly loss nearly quadrupled amid slowing revenue and user growth. \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  (NVDA) off 7% premarket. The chipmaker handily beat 2Q earning expectations after the close but data center revenue rose only 2% from 1Q. With \uc2 Macy's\uc2  (M) and \uc2 Kohl's\uc2  (KSS) both again reporting quarters of shrinking sales Thursday--even though the latest declines weren't as steep as in recent quarters--investors await JCPenney (JCP) quarterly results expected this morning. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0715 ET - Holders of government bonds benefit from flight-to-safety flows amid rising U.S-North Korean tensions in the past few days, but this "sabre-rattling" will have little impact on longer-term challenges for fixed-income investors, says \uc2 Jonathan Platt\uc2 , head of fixed income at \uc2 Royal London Asset Management\uc2 . Especially in the eurozone, government bonds could face a volatile autumn as the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks poised to announce the tapering of its EUR60 billion-a-month asset-purchase program. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos)\par \par \uc2 0629 ET - Nickel prices are significantly underperforming the already-weak industrial metals sector, and are down 0.22% at $10,650.00 a ton. The combination of nickel's high stocks, more volatile prices, and lower base metals and iron ore prices contributes to nickel's fall. The metal's drop is also exacerbated by profit-taking, with fears of Chinese smelter closures having driven up prices by 16% in the past month, an analyst says. "The U.S.-North Korean news flow is seeing money coming off the table across the base metals sector," he adds, referring to the two countries' mutual threats.(david.hodari@wsj.com; @davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 11, 2017 16:07 ET (20:07 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170811ed8b003kv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
