\par\par \b \uc2 US Oil Output Is Set to Rise -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2017\par 17:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1132 ET - Analysts for \uc2 Energy Aspects\uc2  forecast US crude output will creep up and that certain shale-oil producing regions may yield more than the market anticipates. In recent report analysts say that the average US production will rise by 450K barrels a day this year and by around 650K next year. "But the latest earnings season has revealed that the Permian may not be the Holy Grail many presumed it to be," analysts say. "We believe the Eagle Ford, Bakken and the DJ are likely to outperform consensus expectations," the analysts add. (neanda.salvaterra@wsj.com, @neanda1)\par \par \uc2 1116 ET - Argentina's construction industry will expand at a faster-than-expected pace this year and next, BMI Research says in a report. "We have upgraded our expectations for construction industry real growth in 2017 and 2018 to 6.1% and 4.6%, up from 2.3% and 4.4% respectively," BMI says, citing increased government investment in public works. BMI says private sector investment will also be an important driver in 2018 as the government pushes new public-private partnerships. China will also play a key role in infrastructure development, BMI says, referring to agreements signed between the countries earlier this year. China has shown keen interest in Argentina's nuclear and hydropower generation sectors. There is a risk to growth, though, and her name is \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2 , the former president who is running for a Senate seat. A strong Kirchner victory in October's midterm election "could weaken investor sentiment," BMI says. (taos.turner@wsj.com; @taos)\par \par \uc2 1052 ET - Former president \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  is set to best Argentina's ruling "Let's Change" coalition in this Sunday's primary vote, according a new poll by Management & Fit. The survey indicates that if the election were held today Kirchner's ticket would get 32.2% of the vote compared with 28.1% for Esteban Bullrich, who is a member of President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 's governing coalition. Sergio Massa, a leader of Argentina's deeply divided Peronist movement, would get 23.1%. The candidates are competing for seats in Argentina's Senate, though Sunday's primary is a non-binding vote more akin to a poll than an actual election. The candidate who comes in first place in October's mid-term election will get two senate seats while the second-placed ticket will get one. (taos.turner@wsj.com; @taos)\par \par \uc2 1051 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening past 18 pesos to the US dollar. But early Friday, in the last day of trading before a primary election this Sunday, the market was calm. "Remarkable tranquility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, with the wholesale dollar trading smoothly at 17.68 to 17.70," Gustavo Quintana, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader, says in a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  post. In June, investors moved to buy more dollars after former president \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  confirmed plans to run for Argentina's Senate. Her candidacy sparked concern she could regain political clout and hurt President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 's chances of continuing to overhaul Argentina's economy. Some analysts say the peso could slide past 19 if she decisively wins a senate seat in a midterm election this October. In that event, investors would seek financial security by buying dollars, analysts say. (taos.turner@wsj.com; @taos)\par \par \uc2 1043 ET - Shareholders will get a more frequent say on executive pay issues at 146 companies in the Russell 3000, an investor group says. Public-companies generally hold an advisory vote on executive pay structures every one to three years, with shareholders getting a say in the frequency. The investor group--including New York state's comptroller, the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 's investment arm and consultant Segal Marco Advisors--identified 319 companies with triennial votes; 273 this year considered switching to an annual vote and just 127 stuck with the longer interval. At 37 firms, a shareholder majority favored annual votes despite management's opposition. (theo.francis@wsj.com; @theofrancis)\par \par \uc2 1028 ET - Could a new breed of zombie horror be coming soon to your \uc2 Amazon\uc2  (AMZN) Fire TV stick? AMZN announces it signs a deal with Walking Dead creator Robert Kirkman and a first-look deal with his Skybound Entertainment. AMZN has been building its original content clout, scoring big-name show runners in recent months. AMZN adds it's expanding into sci-fi, horror and fantasy programming. AMZN's video ties back to its retail business. Exclusive content draws new $99 annual Prime members, which analysts estimate spend more than double nonmembers on \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 . (laura.stevens@wsj.com; @laurastevenswsj)\par \par \uc2 1026 ET - Tech stocks--among the worst hit in the broad selloff Thursday--are helping the S&P 500 rise with the S&P 500 tech sector up 0.8%, outpacing the broader index's 0.3% gain. The Nasdaq Composite, which heavily weights industry giants including \uc2 Facebook\uc2  (FB), Apple (AAPL) and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  (MSFT), is up 0.5%. The turnaround is helping stock indexes pare declines after escalating tensions between North Korea and the US pushed investors out of stocks, although the S&P 500, \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  and DJIA are still on course for weekly losses. (akane.otani@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1011 ET - While warehouse robots--known as autonomous guided vehicles--have been around since the early 2000's, \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's (AMZN) 2012 Kiva acquisition is what set the industry into motion, writes Loup Ventures. "\uc2 Amazon\uc2  had no plans to sell these robots externally... which created a significant supply/demand imbalance and a massive opportunity for other companies to enter the space," the analysts write. They now estimate autonomous guided vehicles will be a $10B market by 2020. (laura.stevens@wsj.com; @laurastevenswsj)\par \par \uc2 1007 ET - What happens when you spend nearly $1.7B to buy a company, but a bunch of the acquired company's salespeople jump ship? Sales suffer, as medical-equipment maker \uc2 Hologic\uc2  (HOLX) has found with its March purchase of \uc2 Cynosure\uc2 , which makes equipment used in aesthetic procedures. HOLX says publicity of the deal negotiations earlier this year caused high-producing \uc2 Cynosure\uc2  sales reps to leave, and delayed hiring of new reps. The effects continue after the deal's close--HOLX said last week it expects lower sales of \uc2 Cynosure\uc2  products in its F4Q, while it beefs up the sales force. "This is a business where reps calling on doctors and knocking on doors makes a difference. And when those positions are vacant, you see the effect on the revenue," HOLX VP Michael Watts told an investor conference this week. (peter.loftus@wsj.com; @Loftus)\par \par \uc2 0951 ET - Alexa, can you understand me? Loup Ventures conducts a second test of \uc2 Google\uc2  (GOOGL) Home vs. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's (AMZN) Echo devices, asking them each 800 questions, and finds both have improved. Alexa understands 96% (vs. 94% in February) and correctly answers 54% (up from 34%). \uc2 Google\uc2  understands 95% (vs. 77%) and correctly answers 65% (vs. 39%). Most improved category? Navigation for both. While AMZN has a head start when it comes to getting speakers into homes, Google Home has a data advantage that could help its devices understand customers better. (laura.stevens@wsj.com; @laurastevenswsj)\par \par \uc2 0943 ET - The \uc2 CBOE\uc2  Volatility Index shed 6.4% shortly after the opening bell, taking an about-turn after shooting up to its highest level since Election Day on Thursday. The VIX, which measures investors' expectations for swings in the S&P 500, has rocketed higher this week as investors have weighed escalating tensions between North Korea and the US. The index is still up 50% for the week--on course for its biggest one-week gain since December 2015. (akane.otani@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0940 ET - Researchers reported that the sugar molecule cyclodextrin slows the progression of a fatal genetic disease called Niemann-Pick Type C disease (NPC), a cholesterol metabolism disorder. The trial results, published in the journal, The Lancet, involve 14 NPC patients ages 4 to 23, who received cyclodextrin through injection into the spinal column once per month for 12 to 18 months. Patients were assessed based on a scoring system measuring gait, speech, swallowing, and other signs of disease progression. According to the study, seven of the 14 patients saw improvement in their scores in one or two of the categories compared with their baseline scores during the course of the trial. The other seven remained unchanged or had worsening scores. A larger, late-stage trial testing the compound in NPC patients is already underway. (amy.marcus@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 11, 2017 11:32 ET (15:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170811ed8b002cv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
