\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2017\par 16:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1051 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening past 18 pesos to the US dollar. But early Friday, in the last day of trading before a primary election this Sunday, the market was calm. "Remarkable tranquility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, with the wholesale dollar trading smoothly at 17.68 to 17.70," Gustavo Quintana, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader, says in a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  post. In June, investors moved to buy more dollars after former president \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  confirmed plans to run for Argentina's Senate. Her candidacy sparked concern she could regain political clout and hurt President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 's chances of continuing to overhaul Argentina's economy. Some analysts say the peso could slide past 19 if she decisively wins a senate seat in a midterm election this October. In that event, investors would seek financial security by buying dollars, analysts say. (taos.turner@wsj.com; @taos)\par \par \uc2 1023 ET - ING says EUR/CHF may rise to 1.30 in 2018. One reason is that the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is likely to stay still while the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tightening its monetary policy by reducing the amount of debt it buys and possibly raising interest rates. That's because the SNB thinks the Swiss franc is overvalued. According to ING, EUR/CHF is undervalued by about 24%. Another reason is that the two big Swiss banks, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  and \uc2 UBS\uc2 , aren't expected to de-leverage soon, as they did in the past giving a major boost to the franc. "We think broad-based de-leveraging has largely played out now," says ING. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1005 ET - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to look through the recent rise of the euro and continue on a path of very gradual changes in monetary policy, said Frederik Ducrozet of Pictet Wealth Management in a note Friday. "We expect no specific action in response to [euro] appreciation at this stage," he wrote. "We continue to expect the \uc2 ECB\uc2  to reduce the intensity of its monetary stimulus with great caution and flexibility, extending quantitative easing at EUR40 billion per month in the first half of 2018 instead of EUR60 billion," he added. Asset purchases should be fully ended by the end of next year. Still, should the euro stay above a $1.20-1.25 range, then QE could continue into next year at its current monthly rate of EUR60 billion per month.(todd.buell@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0954 ET - The bonds of \uc2 BMW\uc2  may be trading at tight credit spreads but \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  keeps its marketweight recommendation on the German carmaker's bonds. One reason why analyst Sven Kreitmair is unfazed by expensive valuations is the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's support for \uc2 BMW\uc2  bonds. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  currently holds 20 different euro-denominated \uc2 BMW\uc2  bonds maturing between 2018 and 2025. The German company accounts for a fifth of the iBoxx auto index. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos)\par \par \uc2 0910 ET - Treasurys have reversed initial gains after the lackluster CPI report. The market has gotten to a point where it's very hard for it to be altered by economic data, says Jim Vogel, interest-rates strategist at FTN Financial. Fed officials understand that the labor market isn't yet tight enough for rising wages to start pushing up inflation, but they firmly believe that it "inexorably" will get there, he adds. In the meantime, Treasurys will likely remain low but range-bound. The 10-year yield was recently 2.215% vs. 2.208% just before the CPI report and 2.211% Thursday. (sam.goldfarb@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0858 ET - The Fed continues to have an inflation conundrum after the latest data on consumer prices. Overall prices rose a meek 0.1% last month from June, as did core prices, according to the Labor Department's consumer-price index. Over the year, each measure has risen 1.7%. One positive development is that overall prices grew month-over-month in July for the first time since April. Still, inflation looks even weaker when considering 2017 as a whole. Through the first seven months of this year, overall prices have grown at a seasonally adjusted annual rate of 0.9% while core prices have risen 1.3%. This isn't the Fed's preferred inflation measure, but it nonetheless shows a weakened inflation outlook. (joshua.mitchell@wsj.com; @JMitchellWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0855 ET - EUR/USD jumps 0.5% to $1.1828 after the U.S. consumer price index misses expectations Friday. The CPI rose 0.1% in July, still better than a flat CPI in June. But the increase was smaller compared to expectations a 0.2% rise, according to a WSJ poll. Inflation has been coming in under expectations for a few months now, but the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  says the weakness in price appreciation is transitory. On Thursday, New York Fed President \uc2 William Dudley\uc2  said it may take some time before inflation reaches the 2% target. Market expectations of a rate rise in December drop after the CPI release, based on Fed fund futures. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0854 ET - Treasurys strengthen after CPI numbers miss expectations. Both headline and core CPI in July climbed 0.1% vs. 0.2% anticipated by economists polled by WSJ. Continued soft inflation should make it harder for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates and diminishes the threat of a big selloff in Treasurys anytime soon. Treasurys had already rallied overnight on concerns over North Korea, but had pared gains just before the CPI release. The 10-year yield was recently 2.191% vs. 2.211% Thursday. (sam.goldfarb@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0853 ET - \uc2 Dong Energy\uc2 's 3% call-2020 hybrid bond looks like a safer option compared to the same company's call-2023 issue, according to \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 . First of all, the 2020 bond trades at a price of 105%, much lower than 2023's 119%. Hybrid bonds trading markedly above par can be problematic due to the events such as changes to tax rules, triggering par calls. There is little risk to earnings in the short term but further down the line, Dong could face high competition in future wind tenders and a more uncertain growth outlook given political risk, \uc2 Danske\uc2  says. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos)\par \par \uc2 0850 ET - Mexico's \uc2 Cemex\uc2  looks set for a rating upgrade at S&P over the coming weeks, Danish bank Jyske says in a note. S&P put the building materials firm on positive watch in June, citing faster-than-expected financial improvements. European high yield investors, even passive ones, have reason to care, as three \uc2 Cemex\uc2  bonds are part of the iShares euro high yield fund, which tracks the iBoxx junk bond universe. One of these bonds, the 4.625% 2024 issue, is also part of Jyske's list of preferred corporate bonds. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos)\par \par \uc2 0849 ET - US stock futures climb into positive territory after data shows inflation in the US remained subdued in July, extending a weak streak that's persisted throughout the year. Futures for the S&P 500 point to a 0.1% opening gain while Dow Jones Industrial Average futures tick up less than 0.1%. The moves come even after President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  issues his starkest threat yet to North Korea, warning the country that "military solutions" are "in place, locked and loaded." One possible reason why: soft inflation has been a big question mark for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  this year, and investors have bet that continued weakness will keep the Fed from aggressively dialing back easy-money policies that have helped stocks keep climbing. (akane.otani@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0848 ET - \uc2 Dong Energy\uc2 's 3% call-2020 hybrid bond looks like a safer option compared to the same company's call-2023 issue, according to \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 . First of all, the 2020 bond trades at a price of 105%, much lower than 2023's 119%. Hybrid bonds trading markedly above par can be problematic due to the existence of events, like changes to tax rules, triggering par calls. There is little risk to earnings in the short term but further down the line, Dong could face high competition in future wind tenders and a more uncertain growth outlook given political risk, \uc2 Danske\uc2  adds. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 11, 2017 10:51 ET (14:51 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170811ed8b002an\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
