\par\par \b \uc2 Eyes on Argentina's Peso Ahead of Primary Vote -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 187 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2017\par 16:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 10:51 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening past 18 pesos to the US dollar. But early Friday, in the last day of trading before a primary election this Sunday, the market was calm. "Remarkable tranquility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, with the wholesale dollar trading smoothly at 17.68 to 17.70," Gustavo Quintana, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader, says in a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  post. In June, investors moved to buy more dollars after former president \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  confirmed plans to run for Argentina's Senate. Her candidacy sparked concern she could regain political clout and hurt President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 's chances of continuing to overhaul Argentina's economy. Some analysts say the peso could slide past 19 if she decisively wins a senate seat in a midterm election this October. In that event, investors would seek financial security by buying dollars, analysts say. (taos.turner@wsj.com; @taos)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 11, 2017 10:51 ET (14:51 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170811ed8b002am\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
