\par\par \b \uc2 Argentine peso breaks sharp slide after  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 445 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 2017\par 23:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, July 31 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose against the dollar on Monday for the first time in two weeks, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Friday to halt the peso's rapid decline to historic lows.\par \par \uc2 The peso rose 0.86 percent on Monday to 17.65 per dollar, its sharpest single-day gain since April, after touching a record low of 17.80 per dollar on Friday. The rebound came after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $305 million in reserves on Friday, its first sale in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since June 2016.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed July down 5.8 percent. While government officials and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have played down the risks of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  declines against the dollar, the recent drop's speed was out of line with fundamentals and could increase already high inflation, economists said.\par \par \uc2 "Even though they officially are comfortable with (the peso) getting weaker, they don't want to see it falling so rapidly," said Jeffrey Lamoureux, senior Latin America country risk analyst at BMI Research in New York.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float shortly after taking office in December 2015, removing the controls that former populist President Cristina Fernandez put in place to prop up the peso and protect dwindling foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in April it wanted to boost reserves, but it has been hampered by stubbornly high inflation and the sharp devaluation. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Federico Sturzenegger told reporters earlier this month that increasing reserves was still a goal but that there was no time line.\par \par \uc2 The peso had been stronger than expected earlier in the year, and remains below the expectation in the national budget for an average exchange rate of 17.92 per dollar for the year.\par \par \uc2 But with legislative elections approaching in October, in which Fernandez is likely to win a Senate seat, investors have taken profits and hedged portfolios with dollar-denominated assets. A primary vote for Congress, seen as a dry run, is two weeks away.\par \par \uc2 With the depreciation dominating headlines, the drop had the potential to become a "self-fulfilling prophecy" in a country where the public follows the exchange rate closely, said Federico Thomsen of Buenos Aires consultancy E.F. Thomsen.\par \par \uc2 "Argentines have always used the exchange rate as a barometer of trouble, and coming inflation," Thomsen said. "In a country where people look at it, and with the political noise around Cristina, it would be better if it was a little more gradual." (Additional reporting by Walter Bianchi; Editing by Peter Cooney) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-7-31T22:28:20.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170731ed7v00tbl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
