\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina curbs short-term foreign inflows lured by Macri\b0\par\par\uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2016\par 17:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, June 6 (Reuters) - Argentina is pushing back against a tide of short-term foreign investment driven by confidence in center-right President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 's reform drive and big profits.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is curbing foreign purchases of short-term "Lebac" securities, used by policymakers to soak up pesos and contain one of Latin America's quickest inflation rates.\par \par \uc2 The move, combined with interest rate cuts of 375 basis points, is a sign of how much investors' attitudes toward Argentina have changed since Macri took office in December. He quickly cut a deal with holdouts from Argentina's 2002 default, ditched capital controls and let the peso float.\par \par \uc2 Nomura estimates some $400 million in portfolio inflows in the first 4 months of 2016, helping the peso hit a four-month high last month and prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bid to contain the tide by limiting the Lebac purchases.\par \par \uc2 It now decides before each weekly auction which Lebac maturities will be open to investors settling through Euroclear, one of the biggest stock and bond settlement houses, and which can only be settled onshore.\par \par \uc2 In the last three auctions, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has exercised the curbs on foreign purchases and has also cut interest rates to 34.25 percent from 38 percent.\par \par \uc2 "It was the right decision," said Rodolfo Rossi, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief. "Longer term are no problem but... this is obviously consensual interest rate arbitrage, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot tolerate," he said.\par \par \uc2 Macri's election marked the end of more than a decade of leftist and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, and he has guided \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  back to international capital markets.\par \par \uc2 After reaching an over $6 billion deal with holdouts in April, the government, provinces and companies have successfully placed billions of dollars in debt.\par \par \uc2 Investors were lured by eye-popping yields of 38 percent that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began offering on Lebacs in April to help rein in inflation and ease the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide since a devaluation in December.\par \par \uc2 But the peso has since strengthened as the annual soy harvest and the debt issuance boosted demand for pesos.\par \par \uc2 The government now worries the peso's rise could hurt exporters, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is wary of a surge in portfolio investment driving up asset prices and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too quickly.\par \par \uc2 "What we can't allow is when two boys on Wall Street say, 'Hey, let's invest in Argentina' ... and they put the cart before the horse," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Federico Sturzenegger told Congress in May after unveiling the new curbs.\par \par \uc2 He said the measures are designed to ensure the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthens "along with the real economy... and not at the pace of the financial world."\par \par \uc2 Walter Molano, chief economist at BCP Securities LLC in New York, applauded the change and said Argentina "is not just a feed bank for all little piggies to come and eat at, and that is what Argentina could easily become."\par \par \uc2 However, even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is successful in stemming short-term investments, it may struggle to redirect investors into longer-term bonds.\par \par \uc2 "The problem with foreigners is if you want to roll over your exposure to Lebacs, you are going to have to roll it over to 6-month paper and that is exposing you maybe to much more \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk," said Siobhan Morden, head of Latin America fixed income strategy at Nomura. (Reporting by Alexandra Alper and Jorge Otaola; Editing by Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-9T15:08:31.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160606ec66019u1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
