\par\par \b \uc2 Argentine  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to prevent further fall in peso\b0\par\par\uc2 195 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2016\par 22:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Feb 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for a fourth consecutive day on Friday, selling $150 million in an attempt to ensure liquidity and prevent a further fall in the peso, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Argentina President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  floated the peso last December after taking power, prompting an immediate 26.5 percent depreciation against the dollar to 13.38.\par \par \uc2 Since then, it has weakened further to close at 15.47 per dollar on Friday, down 2.65 percent this week alone, due to strong demand for dollars from importers.\par \par \uc2 The former populist administration had propped up the peso with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves while implementing trade and capital controls to prevent capital flight.\par \par \uc2 Macri, a free market believer, campaigned on a platform of freeing up the economy and has unwound many of those controls.\par \par \uc2 But Argentina's restricted access to global debt markets means that if there is strong demand for dollars and exporters are not selling up greenbacks, the peso takes a hit. (Reporting by Jorge Otaola and Walter Bianchi; Writing by Sarah Marsh) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-2-26T22:00:12.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160226ec2q027bm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
