\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 7-Argentina lifts  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  controls, floats peso in bid to boost economy\b0\par\par\uc2 787 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2015\par 03:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds confirmation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Maximilian Heath and Hugh Bronstein\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Dec 16 (Reuters) - Argentina said on Wednesday it was lifting its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls and would allow the peso to float when markets open, setting the stage for a sharp devaluation, following vows by new President Mauricio Macri for reforms to spur economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Macri, a free-markets advocate who took office last week, wants to boost exports and regain investor trust in Latin America's third largest economy, damaged by heavy state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a lack of trustworthy official data.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's previous leader, Peronist Cristina Fernandez, used \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to prop up the peso while restricting access to the artificially inflated rate, spawning a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  black market.\par \par \uc2 "He who wants to import will be able to do so, and he who wants to buy dollars will be able to buy them," Finance Minister Alfonso Prat-Gay said.\par \par \uc2 Farmers in Argentina, a grains-exporting powerhouse, have been waiting for the peso to weaken before selling stockpiles of soybeans. Manufacturers have argued for controls to be lifted so they can import crucial parts for production.\par \par \uc2 Local and foreign investors, meanwhile, might be inspired to bring money back to the country if Macri's reforms succeed in reducing imbalances in the economy in the long-run.\par \par \uc2 Asked what he expected the exchange rate to be when markets open, Prat-Gay said there was "no magic number."\par \par \uc2 However, the most realistic level at the moment was the blue-chip swap rate, used to buy Argentine assets traded abroad. That rate is currently around 14.2 pesos per dollar, compared with the official exchange rate of 9.8275, implying a devaluation of around 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Let's see what happens tomorrow. The policy will be what economists call a 'dirty float'," Prat-Gay said. "There will be fluctuations in the exchange rate but there will also be a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the necessary tools to buy if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakens too much or sell if it strengthens too much."\par \par \uc2 Jorge Mariscal, chief investment officer for emerging markets at UBS Wealth Management in New York, said it was positive to see the new government immediately tackling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue.\par \par \uc2 "The exchange rate was very important because it will eliminate all the distortions that were in the market," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mariscal said there was a chance the peso could weaken more than 30 percent at the start, "but there is also a significant chance that it comes back rather quickly."\par \par \uc2 "There is a lot of Argentine money outside waiting to come back once the new team builds some credibility," he said.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of analysts released earlier this month predicted Macri's policies would spell stronger growth in the medium term after causing short-term pain. The devaluation is seen fueling inflation that private economists already estimate around 25 percent and thereby hurting private consumption.\par \par \uc2 The median forecast was for 3.5 percent growth in 2017 after a 0.5 percent contraction in 2016.\par \par \uc2 ENOUGH DOLLARS TO GO AROUND?\par \par \uc2 In order to ensure there are enough dollars to go around, Argentina was securing various sources of financing and expected inflows of $15 billion to $25 billion over the coming month, Prat-Gay told reporters.\par \par \uc2 A few hours after his news conference, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it had converted Chinese yuan from its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap with China for $3.1 billion of dollars to bolster its foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 Prat-Gay said he also expected the bank to reach a deal with foreign banks within 10 days for a credit line worth more than $5 billion. Last week, a banking source told Reuters that Argentina was in talks over a loan with \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , \uc2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\uc2 ., \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  and \uc2 Citigroup Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Argentina has been restricted from accessing international capital markets for years by a long-running legal battle with creditors over unpaid debt that Macri has promised to settle later in tough negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The South American country has also sealed a deal with grains exporters to liquidate $400 million of produce per day over the next few weeks, Prat-Gay said.\par \par \uc2 Alejo Costa, chief strategist at local investment bank Puente, said the new government's policy move was bold and had good chances of succeeding.\par \par \uc2 "He sent the correct message, that they have the strength to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at certain levels, and conveyed the confidence needed to anchor expectations," Costa said. (Additional reporting by Jorge Otaola in Buenos Aires and Daniel Bases in New York; Writing by Sarah Marsh; Editing by Leslie Adler) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-12-17T03:29:10.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151216ebcg00ocb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
