\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 5-Argentine president-elect taps ex-central banker as finance minister\b0\par\par\uc2 707 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2015\par 02:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Macri comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  management of peso)               \par \par \uc2 By Richard Lough and Sarah Marsh               \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Nov 25 (Reuters) - Argentine President-elect Mauricio Macri named former JP Morgan executive and ex-\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Alfonso Prat-Gay his finance minister on Wednesday in a sign that he will move quickly to restore the bank's autonomy and free up the economy.               \par \par \uc2 Macri, who won Argentina's presidential election on Sunday, named several former businessmen who are deeply critical of outgoing President Cristina Fernandez's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies to key Cabinet positions.               \par \par \uc2 Former \uc2 Shell \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  executive Juan Jose Aranguren will be energy minister and the former Buenos Aires city bank chief \uc2 Federico Sturzenegger\uc2  will be \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head if Alejandro Vanoli steps down, future Cabinet chief Marcos Pena told a news conference.               \par \par \uc2 Macri, who leads the center-right "Let's Change" alliance, has vowed to end more than a decade of free-spending leftist populism and lift protectionist trade and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls that have hobbled growth in Latin America's third largest economy.               \par \par \uc2 But he faces huge challenges. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is running low on dollars, the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is overvalued, inflation is in double digits and the fiscal deficit is widening sharply.               \par \par \uc2 "The economy we will inherit is not in a good shape," Prat-Gay said earlier on Wednesday, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had acted on the president's orders in recent years instead of maintaining independence.               \par \par \uc2 Prat-Gay, who was global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  in the late 1990s before leading Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  between 2002 and 2004, said Macri's government was committed to seeing capital controls lifted.               \par \par \uc2 He said this would be complicated by not knowing the true state of the national accounts, including foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, before Macri is sworn in on Dec. 10.               \par \par \uc2 Macri met briefly with Fernandez on Tuesday night, seeking an orderly handover, but he left disappointed.               \par \par \uc2 "We would have liked to have had a better understanding of how things stand before Dec. 10," Prat-Gay told Radio La Red. "The meeting ... was not as good as it should have been."               \par \par \uc2 Macri named Ricardo Buryaile, a top farming lobbyist and critic of Fernandez's policies, as agricultural minister, a move that farmers in the grains powerhouse broadly welcomed.               \par \par \uc2 Macri has promised to eliminate corn and wheat export taxes and ditch the quota system controlling shipments. He would reduce the soybean exports tax.                              \par \par \uc2 "COUNTRY IS NOT A COMPANY"               \par \par \uc2 Fernandez made a jab at Macri's future government on Wednesday in a speech trumpeting the achievements of her two terms as president, including the nationalization of energy company \uc2 YPF\uc2  and social reforms such as gay marriage.               \par \par \uc2 "A country is not the same as a company," she said.               \par \par \uc2 Prat-Gay headed Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it was hauling itself out of a devastating economic depression and won widespread acclaim for swiftly taming runaway inflation.               \par \par \uc2 He left the bank after losing the backing of then-President Nestor Kirchner, Fernandez' late husband, who wanted more control over the regulator and monetary policymaking.               \par \par \uc2 In the closing stages of the presidential race, Prat-Gay, one of Macri's leading economic advisers, spoke out in favor of unifying Argentina's multi-tiered exchange rate.               \par \par \uc2 He acknowledged this would mean a devaluation of the peso's official rate but said it would not fall below the black market rate that many Argentines wanting to buy dollars are forced to turn to because of capital controls.               \par \par \uc2 "The capital controls don't make sense," Prat-Gay said earlier this month. "They just destroyed local economies, exports and a good part of industry."               \par \par \uc2 Macri said late on Wednesday in a television interview the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the peso in the case of extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings, as is the practice in other countries.               \par \par \uc2 Moody's Investor Service on Tuesday raised Argentina's credit outlook after Macri's narrow election win.               \par \par \uc2 The ratings agency said Argentina's low foreign reserves would add pressure for a swift deal with U.S. creditors, whose battle over unpaid debt has locked the country out of global credit markets.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-11-26T02:41:24.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151125ebbp00gof\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
