\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-INSIGHT-Manufacturers in Argentina starved of dollars ahead of election, hurting output\b0\par\par\uc2 1,000 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2015\par 13:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story first published on Friday, no changes to text)\par \par \uc2 By Eliana Raszewski\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Aug 28 (Reuters) - Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reducing the supply of scarce dollars to manufacturers two months ahead of a presidential election to ensure more are available for the nation's savers, hampering the import of parts and disrupting factory production lines.\par \par \uc2 Tough capital controls mean importers operating in Latin America's No. 3 economy are restricted to how many dollars they can buy each month, while savers have to apply to the tax agency to purchase greenbacks.\par \par \uc2 Two sources in the automaking industry told Reuters that in the first three weeks of August the financial regulator cut the sector's dollar allowance by almost a third. The head of an electronics assembly firm said his allocation had also fallen this month.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and industry ministry declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The timing of the cuts for industry coincides with a spike in dollars available for the wider Argentine population, whose faith in the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  evaporated after a string of economic crises and who routinely seek hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of elections to protect their savings. That is because past political change and periods of economic turmoil have led to hyperinflation, big devaluations and, in 2001, a run on banks.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's actions risk undermining a fragile recovery in Argentina's industrial sector, which grew for the first time in nearly two years in June as an economic recovery underpinned by high public spending shows sign of taking root.\par \par \uc2 PRODUCTION "SEIZING UP"\par \par \uc2 Car factory workers are already working reduced hours as companies grapple with a drop in demand from Brazil, which imports more than half of all vehicles shipped by Argentina, and a sustained squeeze on dollar supplies could force firms to reassess production plans.\par \par \uc2 "The problem is there just aren't enough dollars in the system," said Leandro Liberman, director of Grupo Liberman which assembles washing machines and mobile phones in Argentina's southern Tierra del Fuego province.\par \par \uc2 The electronics sector has a quota of $300 million per month, according to the industry ministry's website. The move to reduce this in August would exacerbate a supply shortage of mobile handset models, Liberman said.\par \par \uc2 "We're not receiving our quota of dollars so we can't bring in parts, and production is seizing up," said Liberman, who declined to be specific about the size of the cut.\par \par \uc2 One of the automaker sources said the industry was operating at 60 percent of installed capacity.\par \par \uc2 "If this dollar crunch continues, companies will have to review their import plans and production plans," said the source, who declined to be identified, reflecting the reluctance of companies to publicly criticize the government.\par \par \uc2 A second automaker source said his company was unable to produce some models because of difficulties importing parts.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Juan Cantarella, who heads an umbrella group of auto parts companies, said the government's restrictions on imports are hurting export revenues. The auto parts companies need to import some of their components and materials.\par \par \uc2 "We tried telling the government its policy is counter-productive, that $20 million in imports can generate exports of $80 million," said Cantarella. "They didn't get back to us."\par \par \uc2 CAPITAL CONTROLS\par \par \uc2 The dollar crunch confronting importers is just one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  headache that the next president will have to tackle.\par \par \uc2 The shortage stems from the country's absence from global debt markets after a record default in 2002. President Cristina Fernandez has had to eat into reserves to finance restructured debt payments, pay for energy imports and shore up the peso.\par \par \uc2 The leftist leader imposed capital controls in 2011 to prop up the dwindling reserves, restricting importers' access to dollars, capping how many dollars savers can buy and preventing foreign firms from sending hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  profit home.\par \par \uc2 At stake in the Oct. 25 election is the direction \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take after Fernandez's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.\par \par \uc2 Leading opposition candidate Mauricio Macri, the financial markets' favorite, promises to scrap state controls on the peso, allow it to float freely and remove barriers to buying dollars.\par \par \uc2 Ruling party candidate Daniel Scioli talks of a more gradual shift toward open market policies. He has defended the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's role in controlling the exchange rate but his advisors also say industry must have access to dollars to bolster output.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Argentine importers are struggling to persuade suppliers that they can honor their bills.\par \par \uc2 Importers owe suppliers $8.1 billion - or almost a quarter of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's total foreign reserves - in outstanding debts, according to the Chamber of Argentine Importers.\par \par \uc2 "It can't continue," said Liberman. "No country in the world can sustain policy like this in the medium- and long-term."\par \par \uc2 REVIEWING PLANS\par \par \uc2 The government appears to have given extra priority to the demands of Argentines seeking to shield themselves from a possible devaluation of the peso.\par \par \uc2 Tax agency data shows that in the first 24 days of August $576 million was sold to savers, more than double the amount sold in the whole of the month a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 "This government is sacrificing economic activity to avoid a bigger fight with savers," said Maximiliano Castillo, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist.\par \par \uc2 The dollar shortage compounds the impact of weak demand in Brazil. Vehicle production in Argentina in the first seven months of 2015 fell 11.8 percent on the year-earlier period, extending a 22 percent slide in 2014 from 2013.\par \par \uc2 Car makers operating in Argentina include \uc2 General Motors\uc2  , \uc2 Ford Motor Co\uc2 , \uc2 Honda Motor Co Ltd\uc2 , \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 , \uc2 Volkswagen AG\uc2 , \uc2 Peugeot SA\uc2  and \uc2 Fiat\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One automaker executive said workers are on reduced shifts in some factories.\par \par \uc2 Labor laws make it costly to fire workers in Argentina and the Fernandez government has also pressured manufacturers not to sack employees through the slowdown.\par \par \uc2 (Writing by Richard Lough; Editing by Martin Howell) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-31T12:00:00.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150831eb8v009yi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
