uc2 \f2 The following table shows the monetary assets and liabilities denominated in US dollars, the main foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  involved in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Grupo Clar\u237\'00\'EDn\f28 \uc2 \f2 's transactions, at the closing of the years ended December 31, 2014 and 2013:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par                             December    December \par                              31, 2014    31, 2013 \par                            ----------  ---------- \par ASSETS \par Other Receivables                  78          75 \par Trade Receivables                 523         418 \par Other Investments                 786         537 \par Cash and Banks                    823         807 \par                            ----------  ---------- \par Total assets                    2,210       1,837 \par LIABILITIES \par Debt                            3,847       3,724 \par Seller financings                   1           3 \par Other Liabilities                  43          32 \par Trade Payables and Other          222         177 \par                            ----------  ---------- \par Total Liabilities               4,113       3,936 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bid/offered exchange rates as of December 31, 2014 and 2013 were of Ps. 8.451 and Ps. 6.48; and Ps. 8.551 and Ps. 6.52; respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Argentina\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the Argentine Federal Revenue Service issued certain resolutions related to the exchange market, establishing regulations on the requirements for accessing such market. These financial statements have been prepared based on the assumption that the Company will be able to access such market in order to purchase the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  needed to meet its obligations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11.1.4.1 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Sensitivity Analysis\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Grupo Clar\u237\'00\'EDn\f28 \uc2 \f2  is exposed to exchange risk, mainly with respect to the US dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taking into consideration the balances disclosed above, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Grupo Clar\u237\'00\'EDn\f28 \uc2 \f2  estimates that the impact of a 20% favorable/unfavorable fluctuation of the US dollar exchange rate would generate an income/loss before taxes of approximately Ps. 381 million and Ps. 420 million as of December 31, 2014 and 2013, respectively. Income from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  agreements in case of a 20% favorable/unfavorable fluctuation of the US dollar exchange rate would generate a gain/loss before taxes of approximately Ps. 21 million as of December 31, 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sensitivity analysis presented above is hypothetical since the quantified impact is not necessarily an indicator of the actual impact, because exposure levels may vary over time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Additionally, even though \f28 \uc2 \f2 Grupo Clar\u237\'00\'EDn\f28 \uc2 \f2  conducts its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Argentine pesos, an eventual devaluation of that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have an indirect impact on its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , depending on the ability of the suppliers involved to adjust their prices to such effect.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11.1.5. Interest Rate Risk Management\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Grupo Clar\u237\'00\'EDn\f28 \uc2 \f2  is exposed to interest rate risk basically through Cablevisi\u243\'00\'F3n, certain of its subsidiaries and ARTEAR. This is due to the fact that these companies have taken loans at fixed and variable interest rates and have not entered into hedge agreements to mitigate these risks. If interest rates had eventually been 100 basic points higher and all the variables had remained constant, the additional estimated loss before taxes would have been of approximately Ps. 3.0 million and Ps. 3.8 million as of December 31, 2014 and 2013, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11.1.6. Equity Price Risk Management\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Grupo Clar\u237\'00\'EDn\f28 \uc2 \f2  is exposed to equity price risk in connection with its holdings of mutual funds, securities and bonds and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  agreements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Its sensitivity to the variation in the price of these instruments is detailed below:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                             December 31, 2014  December 31, 2013 \par  \par Investments valued at quoted prices at closing (Level 1)                  767                163 \par Other debt instruments valued at quoted prices at closing                   5                  - \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The estimated impact of an eventual 10% favorable/unfavorable fluctuation of the quoted price of mutual funds, assuming that all the other variables remain constant, would generate an income/loss before taxes of approximately Ps. 77 million and Ps. 16 million as of December 31, 2014 and 2013, respectively. Income from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  agreements in case of a 10% favorable/unfavorable fluctuation of the US dollar exchange rate would generate a gain/loss before taxes of approximately Ps. 21 million as of December 31, 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A potential 10% favorable/unfavorable fluctuation of the quoted price of investments valued as Level 2 would generate an income/loss before taxes of approximately Ps. 41 million and Ps. 15 million as of December 31, 2014 and 2013, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11.1.7 Credit Risk Management\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit risk is defined as the risk that one of the parties may breach its contractual obligations, generating an eventual financial loss for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Grupo Clar\u237\'00\'EDn\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credits involving the Cable Television and Internet Access Segment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The credit risk affects cash and cash equivalents, deposits held at banks and financial institutions, and credit exposures with clients, including other remaining credits and transactions involved. The companies that operate in this segment actively monitor the credit worthiness of their treasury instruments and the counterparties related to derivatives in order to minimize credit risk. Upon expiration of invoices issued, if they are still outstanding, these companies file several claims for collection purposes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank deposits are held in renowned institutions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No significant credit risk concentration is observed concerning clients, due to the atomization of the subscriber base.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of December 31, 2014 and 2013, non-impaired past due trade receivables amounted to approximately Ps. 398.5 million and Ps. 238.6 million, respectively. These trade receivables are mainly from Cablevisi\u243\'00\'F3n, they are in most cases up to 3 months overdue and involve subscribers with no recent insolvency record. [ 18-03-15 0700GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of the same dates, the allowance for bad debts amounted to Ps. 119.7 million and Ps. 92.6 million, respectively. This allowance for trade receivables is sufficient to cover the past due doubtful receivables.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credits of the Printing and Publishing Segment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The companies that operate in this segment conduct an analysis of the clients' financial position at the beginning of the business relationship, through a credit risk report requested from several credit rating agencies. The credit amount granted to each client is monitored on a daily basis, with reports being submitted to the financial management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The credit risk affects cash and cash equivalents, deposits held at banks and financial institutions, as well as credit granted to clients.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The maximum theoretical credit risk exposure of the companies operating in this segment is represented by the book value of net financial assets, disclosed in the consolidated balance sheet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the purposes of conducting an analysis of the suitability of the allowance for bad debts, these companies consider each client on a case by case basis, verifying, among other factors, if there is any record of delinquency, risk of bankruptcy, insolvency proceeding or other judicial proceeding. Trade receivables comprise a significant number of clients and are internally classified among the following categories: Advertising, Official, Distribution, Internet and Subscriptions, among others.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The companies that operate in this segment have recorded an allowance for doubtful accounts accounting for 4% and 5% of accounts receivable as of December 31, 2014 and 2013, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The companies that operate in this segment did not set up an allowance for bad debts for those amounts in which no significant change was recorded in the credit rating, considering such amounts as recoverable.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The companies that operate in this segment have a wide range of clients, including individuals, businesses - medium-and-large-sized companies - and governmental agencies. Therefore, these companies' receivables are not subject to credit risk concentration.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credits from the Broadcasting and Programming Segment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit risk represents for the companies that operate in this segment the risk of incurring in losses arising from possible breaches of the contractual obligations assumed by business or financial counterparties. This risk may be due to economic or financial factors, or to particular circumstances of the counterparty, or to other economic, commercial or administrative factors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit risk affects cash and cash equivalents, deposits held at banks and financial institutions in a wide sense, and every form of credit granted to the companies that operate in this segment. The maximum exposure to credit risk is represented by the value of financial assets considered as a whole, recorded in the Consolidated Balance Sheet under Cash and Banks, Other Investments, Trade Receivables and Other Receivables.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial instruments are executed with creditworthy banks and financial institutions renowned in the market and for terms not longer than three months. In this sense, the companies that operate in this segment have a policy of diversifying their investments among different banks and financial institutions, thus reducing the concentration risk in only one counterparty.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As to the credit risk related to financial credit, the companies that operate in this segment evaluate the credit standing of the different counterparties to define their investment levels, based on their equity and credit rating. As to Trade Receivables, such companies have a wide range of clients, categorized depending on the type of business. These categories are: Advertising, Signals, Programming and other. Within this classification, clients can also be classified as advertising agencies, direct advertisers, distributors of cable TV, broadcast TV stations and other, each of them of a different magnitude. Due to this diversity of clients, there is not a significant credit risk concentration in this respect.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The allowance for bad debts is set up upon conducting an analysis of the debtor portfolio, which is recorded as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - In the case of individual risks identified (risks of bankruptcy, insolvency proceedings or judicial proceedings pending with the company), for its total value.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - The rest of the cases is decided based on the aging of the past due debt, the progress of the collection procedures, the solvency conditions and the variations observed in the clients' settlement periods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11.1.8. Liquidity Risk Management\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Liquidity risk is the risk that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Grupo Clar\u237\'00\'EDn\f28 \uc2 \f2  may not be able to fulfill its financial obligations at maturity. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Grupo Clar\u237\'00\'EDn\f28 \uc2 \f2  manages liquidity risk through the management of its capital structure and, if possible, the access to different capital markets. It also manages liquidity risk through a constant review of the estimated cash flows to ensure that it will have enough liquidity to fulfill its obligations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11.1.8.1 Interest Rate Risk and Liquidity Risk Table\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table shows the breakdown of financial liabilities by relevant groups of maturities based on the remaining period as from the date of the balance sheet through the contractual maturity date. The amounts disclosed in this table represent undiscounted cash flows (principal plus contractual interest).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Information as of December 31, 2014:           Other Debts \par               Maturities                Debt \par  \par  Matured                                    -          713 \par  Without any established term               2          102 \par  First Quarter 2015                       704        1,961 \par  Second Quarter 2015                      564          230 \par  Third Quarter 2015                       595          372 \par  Fourth Quarter 2015                      203           27 \par  More than 1 year                       3,169          221 \par                                         5,237        3,626 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Information as of December 31, 2013:\f28 \par \par \