\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Argentina's \uc2 Congress\uc2  approves supply bill; business frets\b0\par\par\uc2 552 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2014\par 22:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves)\par \par \uc2 By Hugh Bronstein\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Sept 18 (Reuters) - President Cristina Fernandez's campaign to bolster the state's role in Argentina's economy took a big step forward on Thursday when \uc2 Congress\uc2  approved a bill allowing the government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the pricing and output levels of large companies.\par \par \uc2 The House\uc2  of Deputies voted 130-105 early on Thursday for the so-called supply law. It enables the government to set profit margins and confiscate merchandise from private companies judged to have hiked prices unjustifiably.\par \par \uc2 The vote came despite strong opposition from big business and the nation's key grain sector. The bill has already passed \uc2 the Senate\uc2 . Fernandez still has to sign the measure into law. This, though, is widely seen as a rubber-stamping exercise.\par \par \uc2 Fernandez's leftist government says the law will protect consumers from unfair price rises and stem job losses in times of crisis. The administration has shrugged off opponents' criticisms that more state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stifle the economy.\par \par \uc2 The bill "creates the conditions for regulations by the state in order to prevent large firms abusing their strong positions and holding back stock without good reason," \uc2 Cabinet\uc2  chief Jorge Capitanich told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Capitanich said the bill would protect small and medium-sized companies, encourage investment and boost job creation.\par \par \uc2 Leaders from the agricultural, banking, industrial and retail sectors vow to sue to get the law thrown out on grounds that it violates private property and trade rights.\par \par \uc2 "PARASITIC RELATIONSHIP"\par \par \uc2 In a marathon debate that dragged on into the early hours of Thursday, opposition lawmakers accused the government of suffocating growth of the $490 billion economy.\par \par \uc2 "This government has a parasitic relationship with production and work because it feeds off them but simultaneously wants to destroy them," said legislator Carlos Brown.\par \par \uc2 The supply law is one of a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies announced by \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  since it defaulted on its debt in July.\par \par \uc2 Rattled by the default, markets will watch to see how the new rules are enforced in a country where private economists forecast inflation may top 40 percent this year as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tanks and the economy shrinks.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have fallen 20 percent over the last 12 months to $28.2 billion.\par \par \uc2 Farmers worry the new law will allow the state to grab corn and wheat crops if it decides domestic food prices are too high. Economy Minister \uc2 Axel Kicillof\uc2  said those fears were unfounded.\par \par \uc2 "The state does not want to intrude on the economy, but it does have to regulate the economy," Kicillof told local radio while the bill was being debated Wednesday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 "The government does not have the ability or the desire to control all the comings and goings of the economy, all the prices, or go confiscate grains from the silos," he added.\par \par \uc2 But Martin Fraguio, executive director of Argentina's Maizar corn industry chamber, called the law "a serious threat".\par \par \uc2 "This puts the entire corn chain - planting, harvesting, buying and selling - at risk," Fraguio said.\par \par \uc2 Argentina is the world's No. 4 corn exporter and No. 3 supplier of soybeans. (Additional reporting by Sarah \uc2 Marsh\uc2  and Richard Lough; Editing by Lisa Von Ahn and Andrew Hay) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-9-18T21:23:31.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140918ea9i00h3g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
