\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam markets little changed, Bovespa slips\b0\par\par\uc2 366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2014\par 19:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Sept 18 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks were barely changed on Thursday from the previous day's close, although Brazil's Bovespa stock index slipped before the release of an opinion poll on the Oct. 5 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real weakened slightly, while the Mexican peso, the Colombian peso and the Peruvian sol were all nearly flat.\par \par \uc2 Chilean markets were closed for a national holiday.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, traders wondered how much the real would be allowed to weaken before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The real has weakened about 5 percent against the dollar over the past nine sessions, which contributes to inflation by raising the cost of imports in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Investors have been concentrating their attention on voter polls for the election, with a new survey scheduled to be released Thursday night. They have been critical of President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  for enacting policies they see as detrimental to the private sector and state-run companies.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's Bovespa stock index slipped about 0.5 percent as investors awaited the poll data. Shares of state-run oil producer \uc2 Petroleo Brasileiro SA\uc2  slipped about 2 percent, mostly on profit-taking after a three-day rally, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in Latin America, Argentina's black market peso pared Wednesday's losses, returning to the 15 per dollar level in thin trading, while the official rate stood at 8.42 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The government on Thursday accused speculators and banks of promoting the peso's fall in an effort to destabilize President Cristina Fernandez Kirchner.\par \par \uc2 "With a quasi-fixed official (exchange) rate, devaluation expectations can only increase," wrote \uc2 UBS\uc2  analyst Rafael De La Fuente, citing the widening gap between the official and black market rates and the persistent loss of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 Argentine lawmakers approved a bill on Thursday that will allow the government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the pricing and output levels of large companies, one of a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies announced by \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  since it defaulted on its debt in July. (Reporting by Asher Levine; Editing by Grant McCool) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-9-18T18:45:24.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140918ea9i00kdd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
