\par\par \b \uc2 Moody's\uc2 : Recent emerging market volatility highlights some Latin American sovereign exposures\b0\par\par\uc2 2,279 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par \uc2 Moody's Investors Service Press Release\par \uc2 MOODPR\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014   \par \par \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2  says that recent financial market volatility and pressure on emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have underscored the potential vulnerability emerging market sovereigns face during an extended period of uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 Those with external imbalances, a reliance on external funding, and weak policy frameworks will remain vulnerable to sentiment changes, capital flow adjustments, or disorderly market reactions.\par \par \uc2 Specifically, in the past nine months, much of the emerging market volatility has been in anticipation of, and following the commencement of, the US Federal Reserve's unwinding of extraordinary monetary stimulus (so-called "tapering" of its quantitative easing policy). The volatility apparent earlier this year was also exacerbated by emerging market growth concerns sparked by weakening Chinese manufacturing data.\par \par \uc2 In this context, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  has released a compendium which provides a point-in-time snap-shot of exposures to capital flow adjustments and disorderly market reactions for some of the largest emerging market sovereigns, along with mitigants that support creditworthiness and provide capacity to manage external financial and economic shocks.\par \par \uc2 This compendium, titled "External Vulnerabilities, Exposures, Mitigants and Credit Supports", includes a heat map summarizing key emerging market external vulnerabilities; one element of our assessment of sovereign creditworthiness.\par \par \uc2 In the Americas, the compendium looks at Argentina, Brazil, Chile, Colombia, Mexico, Peru, and Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 To coincide with the publication of this report, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  has launched a topic page entitled China and Emerging Markets - Prospects and Challenges, This page provides a centralized source for \uc2 Moody's\uc2  research related to key credit issues in China and other major emerging markets. The page can be accessed via this link: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.moodys.com/newsandevents/topics/china-and-emerging-markets---prospects-and-challenges/-/007026/4294957592/4294965962/-1/0/-/0/-/-/-/-/-/-/-/en/global/pdf/-/rra?WT.mc_id=PR~EmergingMarkets"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.moodys.com/newsandevents/topics/china-and-emerging-markets---prospects-and-challenges/-/007026/4294957592/4294965962/-1/0/-/0/-/-/-/-/-/-/-/en/global/pdf/-/rra?WT.mc_id=PR~EmergingMarkets}}}\par \par \uc2 According to the report, key indicators of external vulnerability include a large current account deficit; low \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves relative to short-term and maturing long-term debt (producing a high external vulnerability indicator); large government gross borrowing requirements; and high foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated debt as a proportion of total general government debt.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's (Caa1 stable) \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have plummeted to less than $30 billion from $52 billion in mid-2011, as a result of the country's growing macroeconomic imbalances and which has pressured the sovereign's creditworthiness. In January 2014, Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined more than 17% against the US dollar in the space of a week, after the \uc2 Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  limited its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to preserve its international reserves. Meanwhile, \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  has one of the highest inflation rates among \uc2 Moody's\uc2  rated sovereigns, while haphazard policymaking and unreliable statistics undermine investor confidence in the government's ability to manage adverse shocks.\par \par \uc2 With Brazil (Baa2 stable), exposure to external shocks is relatively limited, despite the country's rising debt levels and current below trend growth. Its international reserve buffers are among the strongest in Latin America, and although \uc2 Moody's\uc2  anticipates an increase in the current account deficit to around 3% of GDP in 2014, the bulk of the deficit continues to be financed by foreign direct investment.\par \par \uc2 Chile's (Aa3 stable) vulnerability to external shocks is reduced due to the country's low interest-rate burden and net creditor position, despite its elevated current account deficit and dependence on commodity exports. Moreover, a favorable debt structure and budget deficit of a mere 0.5% of GDP provide the government sufficient financing options to manage internal and external shocks.\par \par \uc2 With Colombia (Baa3 positive), adequate financial buffers and a coherent economic policy mitigate some of its external vulnerabilities. The country's current account deficit averaged around 3.1% of GDP for 2010-12, implying a reliance on foreign financing to achieve balance of payments. In addition, its dependence on oil exports as its main source of foreign revenue renders it susceptible to shocks in oil prices in the event of a global economic slowdown. However, sizable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and improving debt metrics supports creditworthiness.\par \par \uc2 With Mexico (A3 stable), the substantial capital inflows it attracted during the quantitative easing period leaves it potentially exposed to a reduction or a reversal of capital flows. However, an improving current account position, sizable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, and favorable debt structure diminish vulnerability.\par \par \uc2 With Peru (Baa2 positive), sizable reserves of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a low government debt burden increase the country's capacity to absorb shocks. Propelled by strong private sector investment, the Peruvian economy has displayed remarkable and enduring dynamism over the past decade, with average annual growth of nearly 6.5%.\par \par \uc2 With Venezuela (Caa1 negative), precarious levels of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and a dependence on oil exports are exacerbating external vulnerabilities. The country's exposure to external shocks is increasing as its current account surplus continues to shrink, while its capital account remains in deficit. In addition, the economy's dependence on oil as a source of export revenues and persistent large budget deficits make the economy very vulnerable to volatility in global prices and demand.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  compendium - which offers a look at many of the key emerging sovereigns covered by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  in Asia Pacific, the Americas and EMEA -- more generally says that continuing global capital reallocation has driven considerable exchange rate depreciation in recent months and rises in market yields, particularly in those emerging markets that were the recipients of large amounts of foreign capital inflows during the quantitative easing period, such as Turkey and South Africa.\par \par \uc2 Others, such as Ukraine, Argentina and Venezuela, have come under greater pressure, underscoring a diversity of vulnerabilities and exposures beyond just external factors and differentiation by the market regarding its assessment of individual sovereigns' inherent mitigants to vulnerability and credit supports.\par \par \uc2 Subscribers can access the report at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.moodys.com/research/Emerging-Market-Sovereign-Research-Series-External-Vulnerabilities-Exposures-Mitigants-and--PBC_165831"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.moodys.com/research/Emerging-Market-Sovereign-Research-Series-External-Vulnerabilities-Exposures-Mitigants-and--PBC_165831}}}\par \par \uc2 NOTE TO JOURNALISTS ONLY: For more information, please call one of our global press information hotlines: New York +1-212-553-0376, London +44-20-7772-5456, Tokyo +813-5408-4110, Hong Kong +852-3758-1350, Sydney +61-2-9270-8141, Mexico City 001-888-779-5833, S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo 0800-891-2518, or Buenos Aires 0800-666-3506. You can also email us at mediarelations@moodys.com or visit our web site at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.moodys.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.moodys.com}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mauro\u160\'00\'A0Leos-Lopez\par \par \uc2 VP - Senior Credit Officer\par \par \uc2 Sovereign Risk Group\par \par \uc2 Moody's Investors Service, Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 250 Greenwich Street\par \par \uc2 New York, NY 10007\par \par \uc2 U.S.A.\par \par \uc2 JOURNALISTS: 212-553-0376\par \par \uc2 SUBSCRIBERS: 212-553-1653\par \par \uc2 Matthew\u160\'00\'A0Allan\u160\'00\'A0Robinson\par \par \uc2 VP - Senior Research Analyst\par \par \uc2 Sovereign Risk Group\par \par \uc2 JOURNALISTS: 44 20 7772 5456\par \par \uc2 SUBSCRIBERS: 44 20 7772 5454\par \par \uc2 Releasing Office:\par \par \uc2 Moody's Investors Service, Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 250 Greenwich Street\par \par \uc2 New York, NY 10007\par \par \uc2 U.S.A.\par \par \uc2 JOURNALISTS: 212-553-0376\par \par \uc2 SUBSCRIBERS: 212-553-1653\par \par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2014 \uc2 Moody's Corporation\uc2 , \uc2 Moody's Investors Service, Inc\uc2 ., \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics, Inc. and/or their licensors and affiliates (collectively, "MOODY'S"). All rights reserved.\par \par \uc2 CREDIT RATINGS ISSUED BY \uc2 MOODY'S INVESTORS SERVICE\uc2 , INC. ("MIS") AND ITS AFFILIATES ARE MOODY'S CURRENT OPINIONS OF THE RELATIVE FUTURE CREDIT RISK OF ENTITIES, CREDIT COMMITMENTS, OR DEBT OR DEBT-LIKE SECURITIES, AND CREDIT RATINGS AND RESEARCH PUBLICATIONS PUBLISHED BY MOODY'S ("MOODY'S PUBLI