\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Eases Rules,  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  In Bid To Defend Peso\b0\par\par\uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2014\par 08:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 1/28/14)\par \par \uc2 By Ken Parks, Taos Turner and John Lyons\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES -- Argentina's government on Monday tried to prevent last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation from turning into a financial crisis by allowing Argentines to swap some of their pesos for dollars online, but there were signs the move could fall short.\par \par \uc2 Despite a 15% slide in the peso's value last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still burned through dwindling dollar reserves to defend the peso at its new, weaker rate of eight per dollar, compared with 6.9 a week ago.\par \par \uc2 Although estimates varied, one person familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market said the bank spent $135 million Monday defending the peso. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the peso close almost unchanged at 8.01 on the official, regulated market.\par \par \uc2 "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hadn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the peso would have gone to 8.50 within two minutes," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader, who estimated the bank spent $120 million on the day.\par \par \uc2 Worse, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually fell in the black market to 12.25 from 11.8 on Friday, suggesting many Argentines still view the official rate as too strong.\par \par \uc2 "The authorities are rearranging the chairs of a sinking Titanic," said Arturo Porzecanski, an economist at American University, who described the government's measures as erratic and self-defeating, likely sparking inflation that would soon diminish the impact of the weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The stakes are high for President \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  as she tries to avoid a deep recession before she leaves office in December 2015.\par \par \uc2 The government has been burning through reserves to support the peso and pay servicing on some $66.8 billion in foreign debt, according to government figures, which has some investors wondering if the country will run out of dollars this year or next unless it is able to find new sources of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Argentina's reserves sit at $28.9 billion, down from $52.7 billion in 2011.\par \par \uc2 Unable to tap global bond markets because of a legal battle with foreign creditors, Argentina gets most of its dollars from trade and meager inflows of foreign direct investment. But the trade surplus shrank to $9 billion last year, its lowest level in more than a decade, and the outlook for 2014 is hardly rosy.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  long enough to allow the peso to fall quickly against the dollar, partly to protect reserves by narrowing the difference between the official and black-market exchange rates. A weaker peso might also spur Argentine exports by making them more competitive.\par \par \uc2 But the move also comes with big risks, particularly in fueling inflation by making imports more expensive in peso terms.\par \par \uc2 "There isn't anyone not raising prices now," said Jose Mair, 64, who manages a store that sells computers and electronics in Buenos Aires. "I try to sell as little as possible because it is so hard to replace my stock. And my stock is much more valuable than my \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Under new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules published Monday, individual Argentines who are registered with the tax agency and who earn at least 7,200 pesos ($900) a month will be able to buy dollars at the official exchange rate -- plus a 20% tax. Purchases will be capped at 20% of a person's net monthly earnings. No one will be allowed to change more than $2,000 a month.\par \par \uc2 But from the start, there were hiccups.\par \par \uc2 "I got online this morning, before 9 a.m., to get permission to buy dollars and everything went perfectly," said Matias Zopatti, a 28-year-old computer programmer. "When I went to the bank they said they still hadn't received authorization from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars. I'll go back tomorrow and try again."\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 28-01-14 0759GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140128ea1s00058\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
