\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Soft-Drinks Tax Passed on to Consumers -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2014\par 21:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 15:04 EST - Mexican inflation numbers for 1H January show soft-drink prices jumped 11%, suggesting producers were able to pass on practically all of a new tax to consumers. The pricing shows inelastic demand and that the tax's effectiveness in curbing soda consumption may be in doubt, says \uc2 Barclays\uc2  economist Marco Oviedo. Mexico's biggest bottler, \uc2 Coca-Cola Femsa\uc2  (KOF), said last year it intended to raise prices and expected a 5% drop in 2014 demand as a result. Mexico's CPI was up 4.63% in the year ended Jan. 15, the highest in 8 months. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:54 EST - NZD/USD is slightly up as domestic data released Thursday confirmed New Zealand economic strength. But ANZ warns the New Zealand dollar remains a "risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," so if emerging-market concerns escalate, it will "head toward the bottom of its band where it should find support thanks to the strong domestic story." Firm cites that band at 0.8220 to 0.8320. NZD/USD last at 0.8299, from 0.8276 late last session. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:45 EST - The euro zone might no longer be in crisis mode, but the rally in Spain, Italy, Greece and Portugal bonds doesn't mean these nations' finances and economies are completely restored. That fact surfaces on a day like today, when shaky global data fuel a widespread risk-off trade. The selloff in emerging markets is making the headlines, but investors are also quietly shedding peripheral Europe. Portugal 10-year bond yields are up more than 11 bps to 5.02%. Greek yields up more than 5 bps to 8.2%, and Italy's up 3 bps to 3.863%. This isn't to say these bonds can't rally further this year, just that those buying now risk sudden losses if confidence wanes. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 13:49 EST - Flight to safety is the key theme today as the latest broad selloff of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Argentina, Russia, South Africa, Mexico and Brazil underscores growing worries that a major risk for global markets in 2014 could lie in the vulnerability of emerging markets. "If the EM region becomes increasingly destabilized for one reason or another over the course of 2014, full-year risk-asset returns could certainly be compromised," with safe-harbor bonds like Treasurys, bunds, gilts and JGBs the benefactors, says Adrian K. Miller at \uc2 GMP Securities\uc2 . He adds recent soft Chinese data is fueling worries about EM countries, as is problems in Brazil and violent unrest in Ukraine and Indonesia. As stocks sit at fresh session lows, the 10-year note is up 23/32, yielding 2.774%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 13:15 EST - Bids submitted at today's 10-year inflation-protected TIPS sale were only enough to cover the $15B offering 2.31 times, the lowest since April 2009. Even though the offered 0.661% yield is the highest at a 10-year TIPS auction since July 2011, it seems like the lack of inflation in the economy these days is keeping buyers away. Looks like dealers were the most reserved, taking 40% of the sale vs the group's 44% recent average. TIPS have been off to a great start this year as investors put money back to work, but traders say without more compelling inflation data, TIPS may be in store for another losing year. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 13:05 EST - The selloff in Argentine assets spreads to bonds as investors fear the country may be slipping into crisis mode. The 2017 global bond price fell to 83.25c to the dollar from 84.5c at Wednesday's close, according to Markit, after the dollar jumped nearly 20% versus the peso as \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped aside from its usual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollars. "The depreciation is a probably a (misguided) way to try to preserve its dwindling \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," says BBH. Argentina's reserves have fallen below $30B. The firm adds the country, which is already reeling under high inflation, can't afford a devaluation now. "We continue to expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain under pressure, and this is likely to feed into a deeper debt and/or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in Argentina." (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarajan)\par \par \uc2 12:54 EST - Cornerstone Macro maps out a variety of scenarios to calculate exactly how much the 10-year Treasury yield might rise this year. It's model considers inflation, growth and policy rate expectations. Taking the Fed's policy talk at face value, the research firm says fundamentals would suggest a 3.33% yield by year-end. But the tricky thing is "if markets question the very dovish Fed outlook for rate hikes, which seems likely to happen at some point this year," firm writes, looking for 3.5% to 3.75% instead by year-end. Worst case for bonds: investors start to think the Fed is waiting too long, fueling expectations for more-aggressive rate hikes. That would propel the 10-year yield above 4%, Cornerstone says. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 12:43 EST - Capital Economics sees "dwindling slack" in the labor market, and a host of reasons why wage growth "is poised to take off." The firm argues that the declining labor participation rate is a structural (not cyclical) shift and a falling unemployment rate has historically had a relationship to rising wages. It also notes jobs-hard-to-fill index in NFIB's small-business survey is at a five-year high, "and points to what could be a fairly dramatic acceleration in wage growth this year." Capital Economics also points to various surveys showing more job openings and higher quit rates, narrower spread in respondents saying jobs are plentiful vs hard to find and "sharp increase" in small businesses who plan to increase employees' compensation. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 11:58 EST - Gluskin Sheff's David Rosenberg says that in shades of gray, \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  policy is as dovish as it could possibly be within the confines of an official "neutral" posture. Starting its policy statement with a discussion of how low inflation is rather than a summary of the macro scene is just one indication of how dovishly it's leaning, he contends. Rosenberg adds it's rare a BoC policy statement attracts as much attention it did Wednesday, but he thinks that's because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is clearly in a "state of flux." (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 11:54 EST - Prospects of additional Fed tapering next week remain the principal cause of the Mexican peso's weakness, says Antonio Magana at Interacciones bank, while participants use the highly liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a hedge for emerging-market exposure. The peso's use as such makes it vulnerable to any EM spillover from the plunge in Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he adds. The dollar was recently quoted in Mexico City at MXN13.3810, compared with MXN13.3030 Wednesday. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:45 EST - Several emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold off sharply against the US dollar around 10:40am ET, a move without a clear catalyst. Analysts and traders say the \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's failure to hike rates yesterday triggered a loss of confidence in its policymaking, taking the Turkish lira to another record low vs USD and sparking a broader EM rout. Argentine peso was down as much as 18% at one point against the greenback. Russian ruble also tumbled to its weakest level vs USD since April 2009, while the South African rand's at its lowest since October 2008. Dan Darrow of Faros Trading is calling this an EM "contagion," similar to what happened in the 1997-1998 Asian financial crisis. But to be fair, things have stabilized a bit in the last hour. (nicole.hong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 11:37 EST - Two highlights from the Treasury's announcement on floating-rate notes. First is the sale will be conducted at 11:30 am EST on January 29. That is the typically time slot for T-bill sales compared to 1 pm for Treasury coupon auctions. The other one is that the floating-rate notes will follow the model of 10-year note auctions: one new note auction, followed by two reopenings each quarter. Treasury says in a release Thursday that it expects to auction additional new FRN securities quarterly in April, July, and October with two reopenings in the subsequent months of each quarter. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 23, 2014 15:04 ET (20:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140123ea1n00327\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
