\par\par \b \uc2 Hercules Offshore\uc2  Slumps Further After Update -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2014\par 20:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:07 EST - As offshore drillers are notably weak generally, \uc2 Hercules Offshore\uc2  (HERO) is slumping following its downgrade to neutral by Global Hunter, which halves its price target to $6. Going into HERO's after-the-bell fleet update, "we wanted to see meaningful contracts for the Discovery rigs; a tick higher in [Gulf of Mexico] dayrates and \uc2 PEMEX\uc2  recontracting several of the legacy jackups set to roll in 2014, indicating to the market that its newbuild awards are not solely replacement rigs but rather incremental. Failure to see any of these, as well as the lack of contracts for the Resilience and 203, hurts our confidence." It's not alone; HERO skids 17% to $4.80, putting January's swoon at 26% and the stock at a 14-month low. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:49 EST - Flight to safety is the key theme today as the latest broad selloff of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Argentina, Russia, South Africa, Mexico and Brazil underscores growing worries that a major risk for global markets in 2014 could lie in the vulnerability of emerging markets. "If the EM region becomes increasingly destabilized for one reason or another over the course of 2014, full-year risk-asset returns could certainly be compromised," with safe-harbor bonds like Treasurys, bunds, gilts and JGBs the benefactors, says Adrian K. Miller at \uc2 GMP Securities\uc2 . He adds recent soft Chinese data is fueling worries about EM countries, as is problems in Brazil and violent unrest in Ukraine and Indonesia. As stocks sit at fresh session lows, the 10-year note is up 23/32, yielding 2.774%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 13:31 EST - \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  (MCD) has traded in the green most of today despite the broader market's struggles, but looking at the latest sales and it's easy to wonder why. December same-store sales fell 1.2% after flat results a year earlier, with the US skidding 3.8%. Global comps this month are poised to be "relatively flat," said CEO \uc2 Don Thompson\uc2 , a disappointment considering the measure fell 1.9% last January. MCD can get a little solace from the weather whacking results domestically of late, but folks know Ronald and the Hamburgler aren't perform as they hope and have long expected. MCD is up a dime at $94.98. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:20 EST - After slumping 1/3 in 9 weeks, Five Below (FIVE) gets a boost from \uc2 Barclays\uc2 , which upgrades the discounter to overweight while reiterating its $45 price target. "FIVE has a unique and attractive business model, and its growth is still at an early stage. Few retailers are capable of adding new stores as quickly, and we think even fewer have as much untapped market potential." The investment bank seeks to look past FIVE's current-quarter performance, saying it expects the company "to revert to its prior" sales trends. "Although some may view the timing of this upgrade as early...sentiment could turn positive very quickly" as 1Q13 results give FIVE a low bar for the coming quarter. Shares rise 2.4% to $37.40. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:15 EST - Bids submitted at today's 10-year inflation-protected TIPS sale were only enough to cover the $15B offering 2.31 times, the lowest since April 2009. Even though the offered 0.661% yield is the highest at a 10-year TIPS auction since July 2011, it seems like the lack of inflation in the economy these days is keeping buyers away. Looks like dealers were the most reserved, taking 40% of the sale vs the group's 44% recent average. TIPS have been off to a great start this year as investors put money back to work, but traders say without more compelling inflation data, TIPS may be in store for another losing year. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 13:05 EST - The selloff in Argentine assets spreads to bonds as investors fear the country may be slipping into crisis mode. The 2017 global bond price fell to 83.25c to the dollar from 84.5c at Wednesday's close, according to Markit, after the dollar jumped nearly 20% versus the peso as \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped aside from its usual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollars. "The depreciation is a probably a (misguided) way to try to preserve its dwindling \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," says BBH. Argentina's reserves have fallen below $30B. The firm adds the country, which is already reeling under high inflation, can't afford a devaluation now. "We continue to expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain under pressure, and this is likely to feed into a deeper debt and/or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in Argentina." (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarajan)\par \par \uc2 12:54 EST - Cornerstone Macro maps out a variety of scenarios to calculate exactly how much the 10-year Treasury yield might rise this year. It's model considers inflation, growth and policy rate expectations. Taking the Fed's policy talk at face value, the research firm says fundamentals would suggest a 3.33% yield by year-end. But the tricky thing is "if markets question the very dovish Fed outlook for rate hikes, which seems likely to happen at some point this year," firm writes, looking for 3.5% to 3.75% instead by year-end. Worst case for bonds: investors start to think the Fed is waiting too long, fueling expectations for more-aggressive rate hikes. That would propel the 10-year yield above 4%, Cornerstone says. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 12:43 EST - Capital Economics sees "dwindling slack" in the labor market, and a host of reasons why wage growth "is poised to take off." The firm argues that the declining labor participation rate is a structural (not cyclical) shift and a falling unemployment rate has historically had a relationship to rising wages. It also notes jobs-hard-to-fill index in NFIB's small-business survey is at a five-year high, "and points to what could be a fairly dramatic acceleration in wage growth this year." Capital Economics also points to various surveys showing more job openings and higher quit rates, narrower spread in respondents saying jobs are plentiful vs hard to find and "sharp increase" in small businesses who plan to increase employees' compensation. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:35 EST - Noble (NE) slides to an 18-month low as CEO David Williams said in the company's 4Q report "it is increasingly clear" the offshore driller's 1H "is likely to be characterized by lower rig utilization," with that being more pronounced in "floating rigs with limited technical features." While saying NE's exposure "is limited" this year, investors are looking for the door, with shares now down nearly 20% since mid-November. Meanwhile, \uc2 UBS\uc2  drops its price target $5 to $35, saying the sector is "entering near-term bear cycle," with ultra-deepwater set to weaken the next 12-18 months amid an oversupply of rigs. The sector is sliding, led by NE's 9% drop to $33. \uc2 Diamond Offshore\uc2  (DO) falls to a fresh 27-month low on its 4.8% decline. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:25 EST - The newly opened \uc2 TransCanada\uc2  (TRP.T TRP) pipeline could push US crude prices above $100/barrel, BofA/Merrill says, estimating that the pipeline could transport at least 11M barrels of crude oil away from Cushing, Okla., to Gulf Coast refineries by the end of February. Oil supplies in Cushing, where US benchmark prices are set, are at their highest level since July, the EIA says. But rising production and reduced demand as refineries enter seasonal maintenance will limit the rally to the "very short term," the firm says, projecting an average 2014 price of $92/barrel. Nymex March futures currently up 0.9% at $97.64/barrel. (nicole.friedman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:07 EST - Market predictions can be quite the critter to wrassle. Take Mizuho, which says last night that which \uc2 Fusion-io\uc2 's (FIO) F2Q results were "encouraging," they were "unlikely to move the stock materially higher." Oops. Shares of the data-storage firm are atop the NYSE with their 18% jump to a 3-month high and putting FIO's gain for the week at 24%. The investment bank--not alone in remaining in watch-and-see mode--thought investors wouldn't buy in "until we see consistent performance for a few quarters." FIO has been a big spender, keeping margins capped. But it appears things like improved enterprise demand is carrying the day for traders. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:58 EST - Gluskin Sheff's David Rosenberg says that in shades of gray, \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  policy is as dovish as it could possibly be within the confines of an official "neutral" posture. Starting its policy statement with a discussion of how low inflation is rather than a summary of the macro scene is just one indication of how dovishly it's leaning, he contends. Rosenberg adds it's rare a BoC policy statement attracts as much attention it did Wednesday, but he thinks that's because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is clearly in a "state of flux." (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 