\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Bonds Fall Too; Crisis Coming? -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 164 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2014\par 19:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13:05 EST - The selloff in Argentine assets spreads to bonds as investors fear the country may be slipping into crisis mode. The 2017 global bond price fell to 83.25c to the dollar from 84.5c at Wednesday's close, according to Markit, after the dollar jumped nearly 20% versus the peso as \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped aside from its usual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollars. "The depreciation is a probably a (misguided) way to try to preserve its dwindling \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," says BBH. Argentina's reserves have fallen below $30B. The firm adds the country, which is already reeling under high inflation, can't afford a devaluation now. "We continue to expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain under pressure, and this is likely to feed into a deeper debt and/or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in Argentina." (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com; @prabhanatarajan)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 23, 2014 13:05 ET (18:05 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140123ea1n002vd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
