\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Trading:  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Peso Rises;  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Is Cited\b0\par\par\uc2 784 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2013\par 00:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par B5\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 5/18/13) \par    By Ken Parks and Prabha Natarajan \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Argentine peso is rallying after the government launched an aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to reverse the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's slide in the black market, traders say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The peso firmed 12% in the past week to 8.95 per dollar on the black market Friday, a level not seen since late April, according to the newspaper El Cronista, which publishes an average of underground exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the government began to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on May 9, a day after the peso hit a record low of 10.45 pesos to the dollar. State agencies, including Anses, the $50 billion government pension fund, were ordered to sell bonds, according to local media reports.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, authorities allowed a benchmark interbank lending rate to rise almost five percentage points, which put pressure on banks to sell assets.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for the Economy Ministry didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Both measures are a sign that the government is stepping up its efforts to shore up the peso, amid fears the weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could drive inflation, already well above 20%, even higher, analysts and investors say.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value has weakened 32% this year in the black market, as inflation drove up demand for dollars.\par \par \uc2 The government has imposed limits on how much foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Argentines can buy, forcing many individuals and some businesses to seek out dollars in the black market.\par \par \uc2 Lately, the peso has fallen on the black market relative to the official rate of 5.236 per dollar. That has raised concerns that a weakened \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could reinforce expectations of a future devaluation and drive inflation even higher by raising the cost of imports.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Anses shows the government is willing to go out and do battle with the 'blue' dollar and other [parallel exchange] rates," said Adrian Mayoral, a trader of stocks and bonds at local brokerage Mayoral Bursatil.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mayoral said a large volume of stocks and bonds were sold early in this past week and late in the prior week, usually an indication government agencies were in the market. That perception also drove private investors to unload securities.\par \par \uc2 The authorities appear to have simultaneously attacked the black-market dollar, known locally as the "blue dollar," as well as a legal mechanism that investors use to get greenbacks, which is known as the "blue chip swap."\par \par \uc2 The latter involves the purchase of stocks and bonds traded in Argentina, which are then sold offshore for dollars. When Anses sells, the value of those stocks and bonds drops, reducing the return for other holders who are trading them abroad, which in turn lowers the peso's blue rate.\par \par \uc2 Trading in the black-market dollar was virtually frozen between May 10 and May 13 as underground \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers closed their doors out of fear they might be raided by government agents. At the same time, a wave of selling on the stock market helped the peso to firm in the blue-chip swap trade to 9.06 per dollar, from 9.55 on May 8.\par \par \uc2 The weakness of the black-market peso is an unwelcome sign of the deep troubles in Argentina's economy, and raises the prospect of a devaluation to bring the official rate more closely in line with the market rate.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, President Cristina Kirchner ruled out a devaluation as long as she is president.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the official exchange market on an almost daily basis as it tries to buy dollars to build its reserves, while gradually weakening the peso to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 While the government has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the black market before, it is rare for the move to be so large, or to take place over several days, as the most recent measures did, traders say.\par \par \uc2 "Argentina is trying to manage, though, ineffectively, distortion on its exchange rate," said Siobhan Morden, head of Latin American strategy at Jefferies LLC. "Clearly, the government can't control the black-market rate, but they are trying to influence it by coming up with ad-hoc measures."\par \par \uc2 But the government's measures may offer only a temporary reprieve for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Alberto Bernal, head of research and strategy at \uc2 Bulltick Capital Markets\uc2 , expects the Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain under pressure in coming months as the government struggles with its debt and has difficultly borrowing abroad.\par \par \uc2 "The reality is that unless Argentina fixes its fiscal accounts or regains access to markets, the shadow exchange rate will be pressured," Mr. Bernal said.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 17, 2013 18:59 ET (22:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130517e95h006f8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex10