\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Chief:  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Controls Are "Common Sense"\b0\par\par\uc2 By Taos Turner   \par \uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2012\par 16:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES--Argentina's controversial \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls are a "common sense" way to prevent capital flight and keep the economy growing, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Mercedes Marco del Pont said Monday.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Marco del Pont's comments come almost a year after the government began severely limiting the sale of U.S. dollars and other foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the public. She said the controls are needed to stem capital flight outflows--which last year represented 4.2% of gross domestic product--so the government and industry have enough dollars on hand to pay debt and buy critical imports.\par \par \uc2 "It was common sense for a government like this, which aspires to embrace and consolidate this economic process of growth and [social] inclusion had to confront capital flight by Argentines," Mrs. Marco del Pont said at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's annual monetary conference.\par \par \uc2 Domestic capital flight, which involves mainly Argentines withdrawing their dollar-denominated savings from local banks, has accounted for at least 3% of GDP over the past 15 years, Mrs. Marco del Pont said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief defended the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies and said that \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "almost alone" in the world in its pursuit of unorthodox, stimulus-based recipes for growth.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Marco del Pont questioned recent decisions by ratings firms to downgrade the outlook of the country's sovereign debt and the ratings of its companies and banks.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2  cut its outlook on Argentina's B3 sovereign rating to negative from stable, and also lowered the outlook on 30 Argentine banks and 17 non-financial companies to negative.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  based its decision on what it described as a "continued haphazard policy environment", doubts about the reliability of government economic data, and questions about the government's willingness to pay due to a lack of resolution to its debt arrears with the Paris Club.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, Mrs. Marco del Pont said not only have the ratings firms failed to properly interpret events in Argentina, but that people should view their conclusions as evidence that Argentina is pursuing precisely the right kind of policies.\par \par \uc2 "We have to be prepared to see those declining ratings of Argentina and those interpretations of Argentina as an indication that in reality we are heading in the right direction," she said.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Marco del Pont voiced a gloomy outlook for the global economy and saw "no light at the end of the tunnel" for the financial problems in the developed world.\par \par \uc2 While economists outside Argentina are talking about "a lost decade" of economic malaise in developed nations, she said Argentina and other Latin American countries are in the position to claim a successful decade thanks to heterodox policies that stimulate growth and improve wealth distribution.\par \par \uc2 -Write to Taos Turner at taos.turner@dowjones.com [ 01-10-12 1623GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121001e8a1000ss\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
