\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Argentina to Boost Stimulus in 2013 if Needed - Minister\b0\par\par\uc2 598 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2012\par 00:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --Deputy economy minister says stimulus will be forthcoming if needed\par \par \uc2 --Most of the government's financing needs will be covered with domestic resources\par \par \uc2 --Deputy economy minister reaffirms government's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy\par \par \uc2 (Updates with background and comments about government's 2013 financing strategy) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Ken Parks \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BUENOS AIRES--Argentina's federal government won't hesitate to take additional fiscal stimulus measures next year if the economy stumbles, a top policymaker said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Cristina Kirchner has launched a number of stimulus measures this year to goose the country's sluggish economy. Those include tariff protection for local producers, a state mortgage program and lending requirements for banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Deputy Economy Minister Axel Kicillof said the government doesn't think it will need to make the same counter-cyclical spending efforts next year, but stands ready to do so if necessary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This government, if confronted with an [international] recessionary environment greater than that today or a continuation of global [economic] stagnation will take expansive fiscal measures because it has to; it will spend more," he said in testimony to the Lower House budget committee.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government plans to cover its financing needs in 2013 largely with domestic resources, Finance Secretary Adrian Cosentino said at the same event.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Those sources include borrowing from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and public-sector institutions such as state-run commercial bank Banco de la Nacion and the federal pension fund agency. In the case of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the government plans to borrow $7.97 billion from its international reserves to pay creditors next year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Kirchner administration expects Argentina's economy to grow 4.4% next year thanks to an expected recovery in the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Cristina Kirchner's 2013 budget bill forecasts 3.4% growth this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If correct, her government will have to make payments of about 19 billion pesos ($4 billion) on growth-linked securities known as GDP warrants. The warrants pay out when growth exceeds 3.26%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the economy grows less than expected and the warrants don't pay out, those funds will be spent on infrastructure projects, Mr. Kicillof said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are making provisions to pay the GDP warrants next year," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Argentina's official economic statistics have been the target of considerable criticism following a purge of the national statistics agency in early 2007. The Kirchner administration is thought to significantly understate inflation and overstate growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier this month, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  gave Argentina until mid-December to improve the quality of the inflation and GDP data it reports to the fund or face possible sanctions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking on the Argentine peso, Mr. Kicillof said the government will continue its controlled float of the peso to prevent violent swings in the exchange rate or recessionary devaluations that dogged Argentina during the second half of the 20th century.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Argentina\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regularly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, buying dollars to build its international reserves and keeping the peso on a slow path of depreciation against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to help exporters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The budget bill forecasts an average exchange rate of ARS4.53 to the U.S. dollar this year, and ARS5.10 to the dollar in 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The gradual depreciation of the peso hasn't completely offset inflation that most private-sector forecasters say is between 20% and 25%. The government's latest estimate of 12-month inflation was 10%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Ken Parks at ken.parks@dowjones.com\u8236\'20\'2C\u8234\'20\'2A\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 26, 2012 18:40 ET (22:40 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020121001e89q004fn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
