\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Stocks Lower in Light Trading; Bonds Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 338 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2012\par 23:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Merval stock index falls 0.41% in light trading\par \par \uc2 --Bonds close higher\par \par \uc2 --Black-market peso steady\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Taos Turner \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BUENOS AIRES--Argentine stocks closed lower in very light trading Tuesday while dollar-denominated bonds performed well and the black-market peso was little changed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Merval fell 0.41% to 2420.26 in volume totaling ARS29 million ($6.3 million).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Steel producer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Siderar SAIC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ERAR.BA) led the declines by sliding 2.66% to ARS1.46. Earlier Tuesday, an industry group reported that steel production in Argentina plunged last month as factories idled production during a maintenance break.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Raw steel output fell 29.9% to 344,000 metric tons in July from the same month a year ago, the steel industry group CIS said. Output was down 23.7% on the month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Banco Frances (FRAN.BA) slid 2.48% to ARS7.85 while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Grupo Financiero Galicia\f28 \uc2 \f2  (GGAL.BA, GGAL) fell 1.84% to ARS3.20.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares of the state-run oil and gas company \f28 \uc2 \f2 YPF SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (YPFD.BA, \f28 \uc2 \f2 YPF\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) led the gains, rising 1.83% to ARS77.90.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bonds had a positive day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The price of the AA17 rose 0.84% to ARS513.30. The TVPP GDP warrant rose 1.34% to ARS12.90.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The official exchange rate was unavailable for technical reasons on the MAE local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wholesale market. On Monday, the peso traded for ARS4.6045 to the U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Argentina\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regularly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, buying dollars to build its international reserves and to keep the peso on a slow path of depreciation against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since early May, authorities have severely limited dollar sales to beef up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's hard-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, fueling a black market for dollars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar traded for about ARS6.29 in the black market, little changed from the previous session, according to the newspaper El Cronista.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to Taos Turner at taos.turner@dowjones.com [ 14-08-12 2157GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020120814e88e0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
