\par\par \b \uc2 Argentine Bonds, Stocks Rise on Better Mood in Europe, U.S.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shane Romig   \par \uc2 382 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2012\par 00:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES--Argentine bonds and stocks ended mostly higher Tuesday as a more upbeat mood overseas stoked investor confidence.\par \par \uc2 Hopes that the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would come to the rescue by buying Spanish and Italian debt helped boost sentiment, while Wall Street gains added an updraft.\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., Federal Reserve Bank of Boston President \uc2 Eric Rosengren\uc2  said the Fed should launch an aggressive open-ended bond-buying program to boost the economy until unemployment begins falling again. That fueled gains in New York, with the \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index topping 1,400 points for the first time since May.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's benchmark Merval stock index added 1.2% to close at 2,451.69 points amid light volume of ARS32 million ($7 million).\par \par \uc2 The gains were led by \uc2 Petrobras Argentina\uc2  (PZE, PESA.BA), which shot up 5.5% to ARS6.13.\par \par \uc2 Bonds posted mixed results, with buying focused on short-term, dollar-denominated government debt.\par \par \uc2 The dollar-denominated Boden 2015 bond added 0.3% in price terms to close at ARS583.\par \par \uc2 The peso-denominated GDP warrants climbed 0.5% to ARS12.66.\par \par \uc2 However, other bonds came under selling pressure.\par \par \uc2 The dollar-denominated 2017 Bonar X bond slid 0.6% to ARS517.\par \par \uc2 With Argentina's economy heading into a contraction and prospects looking bleak, the time has come to take profits on Argentina's bonds and shift to Venezuelan debt, Jefferies analyst Siobhan Morden said in a report Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the peso weakened to close at ARS 4.5955 to the U.S. dollar on the MAE \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  wholesale market, compared with ARS4.5925 a day earlier.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, buying dollars to build its international reserves and to keep the peso on a slow path of depreciation against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Since early May, authorities have severely limited dollar sales to businesses and individuals to beef up the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which has fueled a black market for dollars.\par \par \uc2 According to the newspaper El Cronista, the dollar fetched around ARS6.23 on the black market Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 -Write to Shane Romig at shane.romig@dowjones.com [ 07-08-12 2213GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5054   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120807e887001b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
