\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch: Risks of Argentine Government Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Remain\b0\par\par\uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2012\par 16:56\par \uc2 Business Wire\par \uc2 BWR\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012  Business Wire. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CHICAGO--(BUSINESS WIRE)--May 09, 2012-- \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Argentina\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government still maintains sufficient fiscal flexibility to avoid direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the country's private banking system, but future efforts to divert liquidity from Argentine banks cannot be ruled out. Fitch Ratings believes that a government liquidity grab, potentially taking the form of compulsory bond purchases, could ultimately lead to a crisis by weakening public confidence in the banking system.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Over the near term, we expect the government to continue its reliance on intrapublic funding, mainly from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Funds could also be moved from the state-owned pension plan administrator ANSES, as well as state-owned Banco de la Nacion Argentina, the country's largest commercial bank.\par \par \uc2 Prior to seeking cash from private banks, we believe the government would be more likely to increase \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in other sectors of the economy, as seen recently in the nationalization of \uc2 YPF S.A\uc2 . Moreover, announced exchange controls and import restrictions should provide sufficient foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the remainder of the year.\par \par \uc2 According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , total deposits in the banking system at Feb. 28 totaled 482.3 billion Argentine pesos, including $13.3 billion of dollar-denominated deposits. Liquid assets, including cash, interbank loans bills, and notes issued by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , represented 42.3% of total deposits. Overall exposure to the public sector among private banks is low, unlike the precrisis period at the beginning of the last decade.\par \par \uc2 We would expect any government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the financial system to be viewed negatively by the public, particularly because the 2001 financial crisis was preceded by state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system and an increase in the banks' public sector exposure.\par \par \uc2 In addition to increasing pressure to divert U.S. dollars, we believe there is a risk that the government may consider other types of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This may include directed credit or caps on interest rates and fees that could hurt bank profitability and the solvency of the banking system over the longer term.\par \par \uc2 The volatility of the Argentine operating environment is already factored into bank ratings. As a result, the current situation is not likely to lead to widespread downgrades of financial institutions. However, any change in the sovereign rating and/or an increase in government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system will be monitored closely and could have a negative impact on financial institution ratings.\par \par \uc2 The above article originally appeared as a post on the Fitch Wire credit market commentary page. The original article can be accessed at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\uc2 . All opinions expressed are those of Fitch Ratings.\par \par \uc2 ALL FITCH CREDIT RATINGS ARE SUBJECT TO CERTAIN LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS. PLEASE READ THESE LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS BY FOLLOWING THIS LINK: HTTP://FITCHRATINGS.COM/UNDERSTANDINGCREDITRATINGS. IN ADDITION, RATING DEFINITIONS AND THE TERMS OF USE OF SUCH RATINGS ARE AVAILABLE ON THE AGENCY'S PUBLIC WEBSITE 'WWW.FITCHRATINGS.COM'. PUBLISHED RATINGS, CRITERIA AND METHODOLOGIES ARE AVAILABLE FROM THIS SITE AT ALL TIMES. FITCH'S CODE OF CONDUCT, CONFIDENTIALITY, CONFLICTS OF INTEREST, AFFILIATE FIREWALL, COMPLIANCE AND OTHER RELEVANT POLICIES AND PROCEDURES ARE ALSO AVAILABLE FROM THE 'CODE OF CONDUCT' SECTION OF THIS SITE.\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings  |               Ana Gavuzzo  |               Senior Director  |               +54 11 5235 8133  |               Fitch  |               Argentina Calificadora de Riesgo S.A.  |               Sarmiento 663 - 7 piso - C1041AAM  |               - Capital Federal - Argentina  |               or  |               Santiago Gallo  |               Director  |               +54 11 5235  |               8137  |               or  |               Lucila Broide  |               Director, LATAM Sovereigns  |               +1-212-908-0898  |               or  |               Bill  |               Warlick  |               Senior Director  |               Fitch Wire  |               +1-312-368-3141  |               Fitch, Inc.  |               70 W.  |               Madison  |               Chicago, IL 60602  |               or  |               Media Relations:  |               Brian Bertsch,  |               +1-212-908-0549 (New York)  |               brian.bertsch@fitchratings.com  |               Laura Villegas,  |               +54 11 5235 8139 (Buenos Aires)  |               laura.villegas@fitchratings.com  |          SOURCE: Fitch Ratings   \par \par \uc2 Dokument BWR0000020120509e859000cg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
