\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Argentina cenbank sees 2012 growth of up to 7.5 pct\b0\par\par\uc2 521 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2011\par 00:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Economy has boomed for years under unorthodox policies\par \par \uc2 * Analysts say high inflation not being addressed\par \par \uc2 (Adds details, context, analyst quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Hugh Bronstein\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Dec 29 (Reuters) - Argentina's economy should expand by 4.5 percent to 7.5 percent next year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday, warning that Europe's financial mess poses the biggest threat to growth.\par \par \uc2 According to it's freshly approved monetary program, the bank said the policies that have helped Argentina's economy boom over the last nine years will remain largely in place.\par \par \uc2 "The biggest source of uncertainty is associated with the sovereign debt crisis and financial system risk presented by the advanced economies," the program says.\par \par \uc2 Italy, the euro zone's third-largest economy, remains at the center of the debt crisis that began in Greece two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Italian Prime Minister Mario Monti pledged new efforts to boost the economy after a disappointing bond auction on Thursday underlined the country's shaky public finances.\par \par \uc2 Investors meanwhile fret that Argentine President Cristina Fernandez's unorthodox and hard-to-predict policies will leave the country vulnerable to fallout from Europe's debt woes and to slower demand from key commodities client China next year.\par \par \uc2 Argentina has roared back from the dark days of its 2001/02 sovereign debt default and shock \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation. Fernandez credits her policies, and those of her late husband, Nestor Kirchner, who preceded her in office, for the rapid expansion.\par \par \uc2 But Wall Street takes a dim view of her reliance on state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained locked out of the international capital markets.\par \par \uc2 INFLATION\par \par \uc2 The country's economy grew 8.1 percent in October from a year earlier, beating market expectations but marking the slowest pace of growth in a year.\par \par \uc2 Inflation for full-year 2012 is seen at 9.2 percent, according to the document issued by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Private economists say that Argentina has under-reported consumer price increases over the last five years for political gain as well as to save on inflation-linked bond payments. They say the country's inflation rate is actually more than twice the rate reported by the government.\par \par \uc2 "These figures show that the monetary stance will remain extraordinarily lax, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continuing to accommodate inflation at a very high level," Alberto Ramos, who analyses the country for \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , said in reference to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's projections.\par \par \uc2 "This strategy is likely to lead to further erosion of the current account and add pressure on the Argentine peso to depreciate," he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  targeted broad M2 money supply growth at an average of 26.4 percent for 2012, compared with the target for growth of 27.9 percent for 2011.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's trade surplus more than doubled in November from a year ago and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  projects a positive balance of $8.9 billion in full-year 2012.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Nicolas Misculin; Editing by Andrew Hay and Steve Orlofsky)\par \par \uc2 ARGENTINA-GROWTH (UPDATE 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LB